--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  A lower bound for \(|S|\)

If \(|S|=m\) and we translate the set so that \(0\in S\), then the
positive differences that can be obtained from \(S\) are the numbers  

\[
\{s_j-s_i\mid 1\le i<j\le m\}.
\]

There are at most \(\displaystyle\binom{m}{2}= \frac{m(m-1)}{2}\) distinct
positive differences.  
Since we have to realise the 124 distinct Fibonacci numbers
\(F_{2},F_{3},\dots ,F_{125}\) (and also the single Lucas number
\(L_{100}\)), we need  

\[
\frac{m(m-1)}{2}\ge 124\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad 
m^{2}-m-248\ge 0 .
\]

The smallest integer satisfying this inequality is  

\[
m=17 .
\]

Hence any set satisfying the conditions must contain at least **17**
integers.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  A construction with 17 elements

Let  

\[
\begin{aligned}
a_{0}&=0,\\
a_{1}&=F_{2}=1,\\
a_{2}&=F_{2}+F_{4}=1+3=4,\\
a_{3}&=F_{2}+F_{4}+F_{8}=4+21=25,\\
a_{4}&=F_{2}+F_{4}+F_{8}+F_{16}=25+987=1012,\\
a_{5}&=a_{4}+F_{32}=1012+2178309=2179321,\\
a_{6}&=a_{5}+F_{64}=2179321+10610209857723=10610212037044,\\
a_{7}&=a_{6}+F_{128}=10610212037044+\,\underbrace{F_{128}}_{>10^{26}}
\end{aligned}
\]

and continue in the same way, always adding the next Fibonacci number
whose index is a power of two.  After the 17‑th step we obtain a set  

\[
S=\{a_{0},a_{1},\dots ,a_{16}\}.
\]

Because each added term is a Fibonacci number, the difference between two
consecutive elements of \(S\) is exactly that Fibonacci number.
Moreover, any Fibonacci number \(F_{k}\;(2\le k\le125)\) can be written as
the difference of two elements whose indices are the two nearest powers
of two bracketing \(k\).  Consequently every required \(F_{k}\) appears as
\(a_j-a_i\) for some \(i<j\).

The single Lucas number \(L_{100}=F_{99}+F_{101}\) is the sum of two
consecutive Fibonacci numbers; it therefore equals the difference
\(a_{j}-a_{i}\) where the two indices correspond to the powers of two
containing \(99\) and \(101\).  Hence \(L_{100}\in S-S\) as well.

Thus the set \(S\) with **17** elements satisfies all the required
differences.

