--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  The Fibonacci part  

For any integer \(k\ge 2\) we have the identity  

\[
F_{k}=F_{k+1}-F_{k-1}.
\]

Hence, to obtain the difference \(F_{k}\) from a set \(S\) we must have
both \(F_{k+1}\) and \(F_{k-1}\) in \(S\).

Consequently, for every \(k\in\{2,3,\dots ,125\}\) the two numbers
\(k-1\) and \(k+1\) must belong to the index set of the elements of \(S\).
Starting with \(k=2\) we obtain that \(1\) and \(3\) must be present;  
for \(k=3\) we need \(2\) and \(4\); for \(k=4\) we need \(3\) and \(5\);
and so on. By induction this forces **all** indices  

\[
0,1,2,\dots ,125
\]

to be present.  
Therefore the smallest possible set \(S\) is  

\[
S=\{F_{0},F_{1},F_{2},\dots ,F_{125}\},
\]

which contains exactly \(126\) integers.  
No smaller set can work, because omitting any \(F_{i}\) would make it
impossible to realise the difference \(F_{i\pm 1}=F_{i+2}-F_{i}\).

\[
\boxed{|S|_{\min}=126}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  The Lucas part  

To obtain a prescribed difference \(L_{100}\) it suffices to take two
integers whose difference equals that number; a set of two elements is
enough, and a set of one element is impossible. Hence the minimal size
of a set \(T\) with \(a-b=L_{100}\) is

\[
\boxed{|T|_{\min}=2}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
**Answer:**  
the smallest possible cardinalities are  

\[
|S|_{\min}=126,\qquad |T|_{\min}=2.
\]

