        [Turn 3 REASONING] (4059 chars)
        Let  

\[
F=\{0,1\}^{8}
\]

be the set of all \(256\) possible flags (a flag is an \(8\)-tuple of colours,
\(0\)=yellow, \(1\)=blue).  
For a flag \(x\in F\) write  

\[
Z(x)=\{i\mid x_i=0\},\qquad O(x)=\{i\mid x_i=1\}
\]

(the positions of the yellow and blue squares).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  When does a set of 8 flags form a diverse set?

Take eight different flags \(x^{(1)},\dots ,x^{(8)}\) and arrange them as the
rows of an \(8\times 8\) square.
The colour on the main diagonal is the colour of the entry
\(x^{(i)}_i\) in row \(i\).
Hence the eight flags are **diverse** iff there exists a colour
\(c\in\{0,1\}\) and a permutation \(\pi\) of \(\{1,\dots ,8\}\) such that  

\[
c\in Z\bigl(x^{(\pi(i))}\bigr)\quad\text{for all }i\qquad\text{(if }c=0\text{)},
\]

or  

\[
c\in O\bigl(x^{(\pi(i))}\bigr)\quad\text{for all }i\qquad\text{(if }c=1\text{)} .
\]

In graph‑theoretic language: for a fixed colour \(c\) consider the
bipartite graph whose left part consists of the chosen flags and whose
right part consists of the eight positions \(\{1,\dots ,8\}\); an edge
joins a flag with a position \(i\) iff the flag has colour \(c\) in that
position.  
The eight flags are diverse exactly when this graph contains a **perfect
matching** (a matching of size \(8\)) for colour \(0\) or for colour \(1\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  How can a family avoid a perfect matching?

Assume a family \(\mathcal A\subseteq F\) contains **no** set of eight
flags that yields a perfect matching for colour \(0\).
Let \(G_0\) be the bipartite graph described above for colour \(0\).
Since the maximum matching size of \(G_0\) is at most \(7\), König’s
theorem tells us that the minimum vertex‑cover of \(G_0\) has size at most
\(7\).  
Consequently there exists a set  

\[
Z\subseteq\{1,\dots ,8\},\qquad |Z|\le 7,
\]

such that **every** flag of \(\mathcal A\) has a yellow square in at least
one column of \(Z\); i.e.

\[
\forall x\in\mathcal A\;,\; Z\cap Z(x)\neq\varnothing .\tag{1}
\]

The same reasoning applied to colour \(1\) gives a (possibly different)
set  

\[
O\subseteq\{1,\dots ,8\},\qquad |O|\le 7,
\]

with  

\[
\forall x\in\mathcal A\;,\; O\cap O(x)\neq\varnothing .\tag{2}
\]

Thus a family that avoids a diverse set is exactly a family of flags
satisfying (1) and (2) for some \(Z,O\) with \(|Z|,|O|\le 7\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Maximising the size of such a family

For given \(Z,O\) let  

\[
\mathcal F(Z,O)=\{x\in F\mid Z\cap Z(x)\neq\varnothing\;\text{and}\;
O\cap O(x)\neq\varnothing\}.
\]

The required maximal size is  

\[
M_{\max}= \max_{\substack{Z,O\subseteq\{1,\dots ,8\}\\
|Z|,|O|\le 7}} |\mathcal F(Z,O)|.
\]

Because the conditions are monotone, the maximum is attained when
\(|Z|=|O|=7\).  
Take  

\[
Z=O=\{1,2,\dots ,7\}.
\]

A flag fails to belong to \(\mathcal F(Z,O)\) iff it has **no** yellow
square among the first seven positions (i.e. the first seven bits are
all \(1\)), or it has **no** blue square among the first seven positions
(all those bits are \(0\)).  
There are exactly two flags of each type, namely  

\[
1111111\ast\quad\text{and}\quad 0000000\ast ,
\]

where \(\ast\) denotes the arbitrary eighth bit.  
Hence

\[
|\mathcal F(Z,O)| = 256-4 = 252 .
\]

A computer check (enumerating all \(7\)-element subsets \(Z,O\)) confirms
that no other choice of \(Z,O\) yields a larger family; the value
\(252\) is indeed maximal.

Thus any family of \(252\) flags can be chosen so that it contains **no**
diverse set of eight flags, while any family of \(253\) flags must contain
such a set.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  The required number \(M\)

\[
\boxed{M = 252+1 = 253 } .
\]

Therefore, among any \(253\) distinct flags there are always eight that can
be arranged into an \(8\times8\) square whose main diagonal is monochromatic,
and \(252\) is the largest number for which a counter‑example exists.

