--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 1.  Flags as binary vectors  

A flag is an ordered \(8\)-tuple of colours, i.e. a binary vector  

\[
v=(v_1,\dots ,v_8)\in\{0,1\}^{8},
\]

where we write \(0\) for *yellow* and \(1\) for *blue*.  
Two flags are different \(\Longleftrightarrow\) the corresponding vectors are
different, so there are \(2^{8}=256\) possible flags.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 2.  “Diverse’’ means a transversal of equal colours  

Take eight distinct flags \(v^{(1)},\dots ,v^{(8)}\) and arrange them as rows of an
\(8\times 8\) matrix.  
After possibly permuting the rows we obtain a *diverse* set iff we can assign
to each row a different column \(c_i\;(1\le i\le 8)\) such that  

\[
v^{(i)}_{c_i}=0\quad\text{for all }i
\qquad\text{or}\qquad
v^{(i)}_{c_i}=1\quad\text{for all }i .
\]

Thus a set of eight flags is diverse iff the bipartite graph  

\[
G_1=\{(v,c): v_c=1\}
\]

has a perfect matching (all colours **blue**) or the graph  

\[
G_0=\{(v,c): v_c=0\}
\]

has a perfect matching (all colours **yellow**).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 3.  A family without a diverse 8‑set can have at most \(64\) flags  

Assume a family \(\mathcal F\) of flags contains **no** diverse 8‑set.
Consequently both graphs \(G_1\) and \(G_0\) have matching number at most \(7\).

For a bipartite graph the size of a maximum matching equals the size of a
minimum vertex‑cover (Kőnig’s theorem).  
Hence there are vertex‑covers  

\[
C_1\subseteq \mathcal F\cup\{1,\dots ,8\},\qquad |C_1|\le 7,
\]
\[
C_0\subseteq \mathcal F\cup\{1,\dots ,8\},\qquad |C_0|\le 7,
\]

covering all edges of \(G_1\) and \(G_0\), respectively.

A flag that is **not** in a cover must be incident only with column vertices,
so every flag of \(\mathcal F\) that is not in \(C_1\) has all its 1‑entries
lying in the set of columns \(A:=C_1\cap\{1,\dots ,8\}\) (otherwise an uncovered
edge would exist).  
Thus every flag of \(\mathcal F\) has 1’s only in the columns of \(A\); all
other columns are forced to be 0.

Analogously, from the cover \(C_0\) we obtain a set of columns
\(B:=C_0\cap\{1,\dots ,8\}\) such that every flag has 0’s only in the columns of
\(B\); all other columns are forced to be 1.

If a column belonged to both \(A\) and \(B\) it would have to be simultaneously
forced to be 0 and to be 1 – impossible. Hence \(A\cap B=\varnothing\).

Every column of the flag must be either forced to 0 (if it is not in \(B\))
or forced to 1 (if it is not in \(A\)); therefore  

\[
A\cup B=\{1,\dots ,8\},\qquad |A|+|B|=8 .
\]

Because \(|A|\le|C_1|\le7\) and \(|B|\le|C_0|\le7\), both sets are non‑empty.
Consequently we can choose one column \(a\in A\) that is **always 0** and one
column \(b\in B\) that is **always 1** for every flag of \(\mathcal F\).

Fixing a column to 0 and another (different) column to 1 leaves the remaining
\(6\) columns free; each of them may be either colour.  Hence

\[
|\mathcal F|\le 2^{6}=64 .
\]

The bound is attainable: take two distinct columns, say column 1 forced to
\(0\) and column 2 forced to \(1\); all \(2^{6}=64\) flags satisfying these
two conditions contain no diverse 8‑set.  
Thus the maximal size of a family without a diverse set is \(64\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 4.  Any \(65\) flags contain a diverse 8‑set  

From the previous paragraph, a family of \(65\) distinct flags cannot avoid
the situation described there; therefore it must contain a diverse set of
eight flags.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 5.  Smallest \(M\)

The largest possible collection without a diverse 8‑set has size \(64\); adding
one more flag forces the existence of a diverse 8‑set. Hence

\[
M = 64+1 = 65 .
\]

