        Let  

\[
F=\{f_{1},f_{2},\dots ,f_{256}\},\qquad   
f_{i}= (a_{i1},a_{i2},\dots ,a_{i8})\in\{0,1\}^{8}
\]

be the set of all possible flags (0 = yellow, 1 = blue).  
For a colour \(c\in\{0,1\}\) and a flag \(f\) put  

\[
S_{c}(f)=\{j\in\{1,\dots ,8\}\mid a_{ij}=c\}.
\]

If we choose eight different flags and put them as the rows of an
\(8\times8\) square, the colour on the main diagonal is the same
iff we can select for each row a different column belonging to the
corresponding set \(S_{c}(f)\).  
Thus a set \(A\subseteq F\) of eight flags is **diverse** exactly when,
for some colour \(c\),

\[
\bigl\{S_{c}(f)\mid f\in A\bigr\}
\]

has a system of distinct representatives (SDR).  
In graph‑theoretic language: for colour \(c\) consider the bipartite
graph \(G_{c}\) whose left part is the chosen flags, whose right part is
the eight columns, and where an edge \((f,j)\) is present iff
\(j\in S_{c}(f)\).  
The set \(A\) is diverse \(\Longleftrightarrow\) \(G_{c}\) contains a
perfect matching for some \(c\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  An 8‑element set without a diverse 8‑subset

The eight flags  

\[
\begin{array}{c}
01110100\\
10100100\\
00101100\\
11110100\\
00001101\\
00010101\\
10000100\\
11111100
\end{array}
\]

( written as binary strings) are pairwise different.
For each of the two colours the corresponding bipartite graph has
maximum matching size \(7\); consequently none of the eight flags can be
arranged with a constant colour on the main diagonal.  
Hence a set of size \(8\) **may** avoid a diverse 8‑subset, so

\[
M>8 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Any nine flags contain a diverse eight‑subset  

Assume we are given nine different flags \(F_{1},\dots ,F_{9}\).
Suppose, for a contradiction, that **no** eight of them are diverse.
Consequently, for each colour \(c\) the bipartite graph \(G_{c}\) built
from the nine rows has matching number at most \(7\); otherwise a
matching of size \(8\) would give a diverse eight‑subset.

By König’s theorem, the size of a minimum vertex‑cover of \(G_{c}\) is
equal to its maximum matching size.  
Hence each \(G_{c}\) possesses a vertex‑cover \(C_{c}\) consisting of at
most seven vertices (rows or columns).

Write  

\[
C_{c}=R_{c}\cup C_{c}^{\prime},
\qquad
R_{c}\subseteq\{F_{1},\dots ,F_{9}\},\;
C_{c}^{\prime}\subseteq\{1,\dots ,8\},
\]

with \(|R_{c}|+|C_{c}^{\prime}|\le 7\).

Because there are nine rows, at least two rows are **not** in
\(R_{0}\cup R_{1}\); denote them by \(X\) and \(Y\).
Both \(X\) and \(Y\) are uncovered by the row‑part of the two covers,
hence every edge incident with \(X\) (resp. \(Y\)) must be covered by a
column vertex.  Consequently

\[
S_{0}(X)\subseteq C_{0}^{\prime},\qquad
S_{1}(X)\subseteq C_{1}^{\prime},
\]
and the same holds for \(Y\).

Thus the sets of columns that contain a 0 (resp. a 1) in the rows
\(X\) and \(Y\) are both contained in the union
\(C_{0}^{\prime}\cup C_{1}^{\prime}\).
Since \(|C_{0}^{\prime}|+|C_{1}^{\prime}|\le 7\), the union has at most
seven columns.  But a row contains exactly eight positions, so at least
one column, say \(j\), is **absent** from that union.  In column \(j\)
both rows \(X\) and \(Y\) have the same colour (otherwise \(j\) would
belong to the appropriate \(C_{c}^{\prime}\)).  Call this colour \(c\).

Now consider the eight‑set obtained by deleting the *other* row
(\(Y\) if we keep \(X\)).  In this eight‑set the colour \(c\) occurs
in column \(j\) for the remaining seven rows, and also in row \(X\)
itself (by the definition of \(c\)).  Hence the eight rows together
contain the colour \(c\) in eight distinct columns – namely the seven
columns of \(S_{c}(X)\) together with the column \(j\).  This yields a
perfect matching of colour \(c\) in the corresponding bipartite graph,
i.e. a diverse eight‑subset, contradicting the assumption.

Therefore any collection of nine distinct flags must contain eight
flags that can be arranged with a constant colour on the main diagonal.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  The smallest such integer  

We have exhibited a set of eight flags with no diverse eight‑subset,
and we have proved that nine flags always contain a diverse eight‑subset.
Consequently

\[
\boxed{M=9}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
**Answer:** The smallest integer \(M\) with the required property is
\(M=9\).

