        Let  

\[
F\subseteq\{0,1\}^{8}
\]

be a set of flags (a flag is an 8‑tuple of colours, 0 = blue, 1 = yellow).  
For a colour \(c\in\{0,1\}\) consider the bipartite graph  

\[
G_{c}= (F,\;[8];\;E_c),\qquad 
(i,j)\in E_c\iff \text{the }j\text{‑th field of flag }i\text{ has colour }c .
\]

A set of 8 flags is *diverse* iff for some colour \(c\) the graph \(G_c\) contains a
matching of size \(8\) (the matched column of a flag is the diagonal position of that
flag).  
Thus a family \(F\) **does not** contain a diverse set iff for both colours
\(c=0,1\) the maximum matching of \(G_c\) has size at most \(7\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  A family of size 64 without a diverse set  

Fix the first field to be blue and the second field to be yellow.
All flags satisfying  

\[
x_1=0,\qquad x_2=1
\]

form a family  

\[
\mathcal{A}= \{(0,1,x_3,\dots ,x_8):x_i\in\{0,1\}\},
\qquad |\mathcal{A}|=2^{6}=64 .
\]

In \(\mathcal{A}\) every flag has a 1 in column 2 and a 0 in column 1,
so in the graphs \(G_0\) and \(G_1\) the columns \(\{1\}\) and \(\{2\}\) are
already covered; the remaining six columns can give at most a matching of size
\(6\). Consequently the maximum matching in each of \(G_0,G_1\) is \(7\) and
\(\mathcal{A}\) contains no diverse set.

Hence a set of \(64\) flags may avoid a diverse subset.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Upper bound – no family larger than 64 can avoid a diverse set  

Assume a family \(F\) contains **no** diverse set.
For a colour \(c\) let \(\nu_c\) be the size of a maximum matching of \(G_c\).
Because a diverse set would give a matching of size \(8\),

\[
\nu_0\le 7,\qquad \nu_1\le 7 .
\]

By König’s theorem, for each colour there exists a vertex‑cover of size
\(\nu_c\).  Choose such minimum covers

\[
C_1\subseteq F\cup[8],\qquad |C_1|\le 7,\qquad
C_0\subseteq F\cup[8],\qquad |C_0|\le 7 .
\]

Write  

\[
X = C_1\cap[8],\qquad Y = C_0\cap[8]
\]

for the column vertices that belong to the two covers.
If a column \(j\notin X\) then every flag of \(F\) has a **0** in column \(j\);
otherwise the edge \((\text{flag},j)\) of colour 1 would be uncovered.
Similarly, if \(j\notin Y\) then every flag has a **1** in column \(j\).

Consequently  

\[
X\cup Y = [8] .\tag{1}
\]

Indeed, if a column \(j\) were missing from both \(X\) and \(Y\), a flag would
have to be simultaneously 0 and 1 in that column – impossible.

Now consider any flag \(f\in F\).  
* If \(j\in X\setminus Y\) then \(f_j=1\) (forced by the previous paragraph).  
* If \(j\in Y\setminus X\) then \(f_j=0\).  
* If \(j\in X\cap Y\) the colour of \(f_j\) is unrestricted.

Thus every flag is completely determined by its entries in the
intersection \(X\cap Y\); the other six columns are fixed for **all** flags.
Hence

\[
|F|\le 2^{|X\cap Y|}.
\]

From (1) we have  

\[
|X\cap Y| = |X|+|Y|-8\le 7+7-8 = 6 .
\]

Therefore  

\[
|F|\le 2^{6}=64 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  The required number \(M\)

The largest possible size of a set of flags that contains **no** diverse
subset is \(64\).  Consequently, any collection of \(64+1\) distinct flags
must contain a diverse set of eight flags.

\[
\boxed{M=65}
\]

