        \[
\boxed{M=65}
\]

**Explanation**

A flag is an \(8\)-tuple of colours, i.e. a binary word of length \(8\).
For a colour \(c\in\{0,1\}\) (yellow = 0, blue = 1) consider the bipartite
graph  

* left side – the eight positions \(1,\dots ,8\);
* right side – the chosen flags;
* an edge \((i,F)\) exists iff the \(i\)-th field of flag \(F\) has colour \(c\).

A set of eight flags can be arranged into an \(8\times8\) square with a
monochromatic main diagonal of colour \(c\) **iff** the graph for colour \(c\)
contains a perfect matching (Hall’s marriage theorem).

Thus a family of flags **does not** contain a diverse set exactly when,
for both colours \(c=0\) and \(c=1\), the corresponding bipartite graph has
no perfect matching.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  A bound for a family without a perfect matching

Assume a family \(\mathcal F\) of flags has **no** perfect matching for colour
\(0\).  
By Hall’s theorem there is a non‑empty set of positions
\(S_0\subseteq\{1,\dots ,8\}\) with  

\[
|N_0(S_0)|\le |S_0|-1,
\]

where \(N_0(S_0)\) is the set of flags that have a \(0\) in at least one
position of \(S_0\).
Hence at most \(|S_0|-1\) flags of \(\mathcal F\) contain a \(0\) in a column
of \(S_0\); all the remaining flags are forced to have a \(1\) in **every**
column of \(S_0\).

Define analogously a set \(S_1\) (possibly different) for colour \(1\):
at most \(|S_1|-1\) flags contain a \(1\) in a column of \(S_1\); the other
flags are all \(0\) in each column of \(S_1\).

Consequently every flag of \(\mathcal F\) belongs to one of the three
disjoint groups  

* \(A\): flags with a \(0\) in \(S_0\) (\(|A|\le |S_0|-1\));
* \(B\): flags with a \(1\) in \(S_1\) (\(|B|\le |S_1|-1\));
* \(C\): flags having **no** \(0\) in \(S_0\) **and** no \(1\) in \(S_1\).

A flag of type \(C\) must be \(1\) on every column of \(S_0\) and \(0\) on
every column of \(S_1\).  
If \(S_0\cap S_1\neq\varnothing\) such a flag cannot exist, so \(|C|=0\).
If the two sets are disjoint, the entries on the remaining
\(8-|S_0|-|S_1|\) columns are free, giving

\[
|C|\le 2^{\,8-|S_0|-|S_1|}.
\]

Hence, for any family without a diverse set,

\[
|\mathcal F|\le (|S_0|-1)+(|S_1|-1)+2^{\,8-|S_0|-|S_1|}.
\tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Maximising the right‑hand side

Let \(k=|S_0|\) and \(\ell=|S_1|\) (\(1\le k,\ell\le 8\)).
If \(k+\ell>8\) the term \(2^{8-k-\ell}\) disappears, and (1) becomes
\(k+\ell-2\le 14\), far below the needed size.
Thus the maximum occurs when the two sets are disjoint, i.e. \(k+\ell\le8\).

Define  

\[
f(k,\ell)= (k-1)+(\ell-1)+2^{\,8-k-\ell},\qquad
1\le k,\ell,\;k+\ell\le8 .
\]

A short check (by hand or computer) shows that the maximum of \(f\) is

\[
f(1,1)=0+0+2^{6}=64 .
\]

All other pairs \((k,\ell)\) give a smaller value.  
Therefore any family of **more than 64** flags must contain a perfect
matching for colour 0 or for colour 1, i.e. a diverse set of eight flags.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  A construction of size 64 without a diverse set

Take  

\[
S_0=\{1\},\qquad S_1=\{2\}.
\]

Consider all flags whose first column is blue (1) and second column is yellow
(0); the remaining six columns are arbitrary.  
There are \(2^{6}=64\) such flags.

* No flag has a \(0\) in column 1, so the colour‑0 graph has no edge incident
to vertex 1; consequently it cannot have a perfect matching.
* No flag has a \(1\) in column 2, so the colour‑1 graph lacks an edge at
vertex 2 and also cannot have a perfect matching.

Thus this set of 64 flags contains **no** diverse subset, proving that the
bound \(64\) is attainable.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Conclusion

The largest possible size of a family of distinct flags without a diverse
subset is \(64\). Consequently the smallest integer \(M\) that guarantees a
diverse set among any \(M\) flags is

\[
\boxed{M=65}.
\]

