        Let  

\[
\mathcal{F}\subseteq\{0,1\}^{8}
\]

be a set of different flags (a flag is an 8‑tuple of colours, 0 = yellow,
1 = blue).  
For a colour \(c\in\{0,1\}\) and a column \(i\in\{1,\dots ,8\}\) put  

\[
A_i^{(c)}=\{\,f\in\mathcal{F}\mid f_i=c\,\}.
\]

If we choose eight flags and put them as rows of an \(8\times 8\) square,
the colour on the main diagonal is the same \(c\) iff we can select
different flags \(f^{(1)},\dots ,f^{(8)}\) such that  

\[
f^{(i)}\in A_i^{(c)}\qquad(i=1,\dots ,8).
\]

Thus a set of eight flags is **diverse** exactly when the bipartite graph
with left part \(\{1,\dots ,8\}\) (the columns) and right part
\(\mathcal{F}\) and an edge \(i\!-\!f\) whenever \(f_i=c\) contains a
perfect matching.  
By Hall’s marriage theorem this is equivalent to  

\[
\tag{H_c}
\forall X\subseteq\{1,\dots ,8\}\;:\;
\bigl|\,\bigcup_{i\in X}A_i^{(c)}\,\bigr|\ge |X|.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  How large a family can avoid a diverse set?

Assume that \(\mathcal{F}\) contains **no** diverse set.  
Then for each colour \(c\) condition \((H_c)\) fails, i.e. there is a
non‑empty column set \(X_c\) with

\[
\bigl|\,\bigcup_{i\in X_c}A_i^{(c)}\,\bigr|\le |X_c|-1. \tag{1}
\]

Denote  

\[
B_c=\bigcup_{i\in X_c}A_i^{(c)}\qquad (c=0,1).
\]

All flags of \(\mathcal{F}\) belong to one of the four classes  

\[
\begin{aligned}
&\mathcal{N}= \mathcal{F}\setminus (B_0\cup B_1),\\
&\mathcal{U}_0 = B_0\setminus B_1,\qquad
\mathcal{U}_1 = B_1\setminus B_0,\\
&\mathcal{I}= B_0\cap B_1 .
\end{aligned}
\]

Let  

\[
n_0=|X_0|,\qquad n_1=|X_1|.
\]

From (1) we have  

\[
|B_0|\le n_0-1,\qquad |B_1|\le n_1-1. \tag{2}
\]

Consequently  

\[
|\mathcal{F}| = |\mathcal{N}|+|\mathcal{U}_0|+|\mathcal{U}_1|+|\mathcal{I}|
                = 256-|(B_0\cup B_1)|
                \le 256-(|B_0|+|B_1|-|\mathcal{I}|).
\]

To maximise \(|\mathcal{F}|\) we want the right‑hand side as large as
possible, i.e. we want \(|B_0|\) and \(|B_1|\) as small as allowed by
(2) and we want the intersection \(\mathcal{I}\) to be as large as
possible.  
The intersection consists exactly of those flags that have colour \(0\)
in at least one column of \(X_0\) **and** colour \(1\) in at least one
column of \(X_1\).

If \(|X_0|\ge 2\) then fixing the columns of \(X_0\) already forces at
least one column to be \(0\); similarly \(|X_1|\ge 2\) forces at least
one column to be \(1\).  Hence each additional column in \(X_0\) or
\(X_1\) reduces the number of free positions by at least one, and the
total number of admissible flags is at most  

\[
2^{8-(|X_0|+|X_1|)}\le 2^{6}=64 .
\]

The same bound is obtained when one of the sets has size \(1\) and the
other has size \(\ge 2\).  

The **largest** possible family is obtained when both \(X_0\) and
\(X_1\) consist of a single column, say \(X_0=\{1\}, X_1=\{2\}\).
Then (1) forces

* no flag may have a \(0\) in column 1,
* no flag may have a \(1\) in column 2.

All remaining six columns are unrestricted, giving exactly  

\[
2^{6}=64
\]

different flags.  This family indeed contains no diverse set, because
column 1 never contains a \(0\) and column 2 never contains a \(1\), so
neither colour can appear on the whole main diagonal.

Thus

\[
\boxed{\text{the maximum size of a set of flags without a diverse
subset is }64 } .
\]

