--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 1.  Flags and the two colour‑graphs  

Write a flag as an 8‑tuple  

\[
f=(f_{1},f_{2},\dots ,f_{8}),\qquad f_{i}\in\{Y,B\}.
\]

For a colour \(c\in\{Y,B\}\) consider the bipartite graph  

\[
G_{c}= (F,\,\{1,\dots ,8\},\,E_{c}),
\qquad 
(i,j)\in E_{c}\Longleftrightarrow f_{j}=c .
\]

A set of 8 flags can be arranged in a diverse square with colour \(c\) on the
main diagonal **iff** the graph \(G_{c}\) contains a matching that covers all
8 flag‑vertices; i.e. a perfect matching of size 8.
Thus a family \(\mathcal F\) of flags contains no diverse 8‑set
iff for **both** colours the maximum matching size of \(G_{c}\) is at most
\(7\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 2.  A family of 64 flags with no diverse set  

Fix the first two positions of every flag:

\[
f_{1}=B,\qquad f_{2}=Y .
\]

The remaining six positions are arbitrary, giving \(2^{6}=64\) different flags.
In this family

* column 1 is always **B**, hence it covers all \(B\)-edges;
* column 2 is always **Y**, hence it covers all \(Y\)-edges.

Consequently each colour‑graph has a vertex cover consisting of a single
column, so the maximum matching size of both \(G_{Y}\) and \(G_{B}\) is at most
\(7\).  
Hence the 64 flags above contain no diverse 8‑set.

Therefore  

\[
M\ge 64+1=65 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 3.  Any 65 flags contain a diverse set  

Assume that a collection \(\mathcal F\) of \(65\) distinct flags has **no**
diverse 8‑set.  
Then for each colour \(c\) the graph \(G_{c}\) has a matching of size at most
\(7\).  
By König’s theorem the size of a minimum vertex cover of \(G_{c}\) equals the
size of a maximum matching; consequently each \(G_{c}\) possesses a vertex
cover \(C_{c}\) with  

\[
|C_{c}|\le 7 .
\]

Write a cover as the union of a set of columns and a set of flags.
Let  

\[
A_c = C_c\cap\{1,\dots ,8\}\qquad\text{(columns)} ,
\qquad
F_c = C_c\setminus A_c\qquad\text{(flags)} .
\]

Hence  

\[
|A_c|+|F_c|\le 7 .\tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
#### 3.1  A column that is uncovered for both colours  

If a flag \(f\) is **not** in \(F_Y\) then every \(Y\)-edge incident with \(f\)
must be incident with a column of \(A_Y\); consequently all its \(Y\)-positions
lie inside \(A_Y\).  
Analogously, a flag not belonging to \(F_B\) has all its \(B\)-positions inside
\(A_B\).

If a column \(j\) belongs to neither \(A_Y\) nor \(A_B\) then

* every flag outside \(F_Y\) is **B** in column \(j\);
* every flag outside \(F_B\) is **Y** in column \(j\).

Thus a flag that is outside both \(F_Y\) and \(F_B\) would have to be simultaneously
\(Y\) and \(B\) in column \(j\), an impossibility.  
Hence every flag of \(\mathcal F\) lies in at least one of the two flag‑parts:

\[
\mathcal F\subseteq F_Y\cup F_B .\tag{2}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
#### 3.2  Bounding the number of flags  

From (1) we have \(|F_Y|\le 7\) and \(|F_B|\le 7\).  
Using (2),

\[
|\mathcal F|\le |F_Y|+|F_B|\le 7+7=14 .
\]

This contradicts \(|\mathcal F|=65\).  
The contradiction arose from the assumption that no diverse 8‑set exists,
therefore any family of \(65\) distinct flags **must** contain a diverse set.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 4.  Conclusion  

* There exists a family of \(64\) flags with no diverse 8‑set, so
  \(M\ge 65\).
* Every family of \(65\) flags necessarily contains a diverse 8‑set, so
  \(M\le 65\).

Hence the smallest integer with the required property is  

\[
\boxed{M=65}.
\]

