        Let a flag be represented by an 8‑bit word  

\[
f=(f_1,f_2,\dots ,f_8),\qquad f_i\in\{0,1\},
\]

where \(0\) denotes yellow and \(1\) denotes blue.
For a colour \(c\in\{0,1\}\) draw the bipartite graph  

\[
G_c\;:\; \text{left vertices }= \text{chosen flags},\qquad
\text{right vertices }=\{1,\dots ,8\},
\]

and join a flag \(f\) with column \(i\) iff \(f_i=c\).

A set of 8 flags is *diverse* exactly when, for one of the colours
\(c\), the graph \(G_c\) contains a matching of size \(8\) (the
matching gives the column that each flag uses on the main diagonal).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  An upper bound for a family without a diverse 8‑set  

Assume a family \(\mathcal F\) of flags contains **no** diverse 8‑set.
Then for both colours the maximum matching size is at most \(7\).
By König’s theorem the size of a minimum vertex‑cover of \(G_c\) equals
the size of a maximum matching, hence each colour possesses a vertex‑
cover of size at most \(7\).

The right side of the bipartite graph consists of the eight columns.
Consequently, for each colour there is at least one column that does **not**
belong to the cover.  

* For colour \(0\) (yellow) there is a column \(a\) that is not in the
cover; therefore no edge of colour \(0\) is incident with column \(a\),
i.e. **every flag of \(\mathcal F\) is yellow in column \(a\).**

* For colour \(1\) (blue) there is a column \(b\) (different from \(a\))
that is not in its cover; thus **every flag of \(\mathcal F\) is blue in
column \(b\).**

Hence every flag in \(\mathcal F\) has the fixed pattern  

\[
\text{column }a\;=\;\text{yellow},\qquad
\text{column }b\;=\;\text{blue}.
\]

The remaining six columns are unrestricted, so at most  

\[
2^{6}=64
\]

different flags can satisfy this pattern. Therefore  

\[
|\mathcal F|\le 64 .
\tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  A family of size 64 without a diverse set  

Fix two distinct columns, say column \(1\) and column \(3\).
Take **all** flags that are yellow in column 1 and blue in column 3.
There are \(2^{6}=64\) such flags.  

For colour yellow the only column that can be used by all flags is column 1,
so the maximum matching in \(G_{0}\) has size \(7\); the same holds for
colour blue (column 3). Consequently this family contains no diverse
8‑set, showing that the bound (1) is attainable.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Determination of \(M\)

From (1) any family of \(65\) distinct flags must contain a diverse set
of eight flags, while the construction above shows that a family of
\(64\) flags may avoid it. Hence the smallest integer with the required
property is  

\[
\boxed{M=65}.
\]

