--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 1.  Flags as binary strings  

Write a flag as a binary word of length \(8\);  
the \(i\)-th field (counted from the left) is the \(i\)-th digit,
\(0\) = yellow, \(1\) = blue.  
Thus the set of all flags is \(\{0,1\}^{8}\) ( \(2^{8}=256\) elements).

For a set \(F\subset\{0,1\}^{8}\) and a colour \(c\in\{0,1\}\) consider the
bipartite graph  

\[
G_c(F)=\bigl(\{1,\dots ,8\},F;E_c\bigr),\qquad
(i,s)\in E_c\iff s_i=c .
\]

A set of eight flags can be placed in an \(8\times 8\) square with a
monochromatic main diagonal of colour \(c\) **iff**
\(G_c(F)\) has a perfect matching (Kőnig–Hall theorem).

Hence a set \(F\) is *diverse* exactly when for one of the colours
\(c=0,1\) the graph \(G_c(F)\) possesses a perfect matching.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 2.  A 9‑element family without a diverse 8‑subset  

The following nine flags (written in binary) contain **no** diverse
8‑subset:

\[
\begin{aligned}
&00000000,\;00000001,\;00000010,\;00000011,\;00000100,\\
&00111111,\;01111111,\;10111111,\;11111111 .
\end{aligned}
\]

(Decimal numbers: \(0,1,2,3,4,63,127,191,255\).)

For every colour \(c\) the graph \(G_c\) has a vertex cover of size
\(7\); consequently the maximum matching size is at most \(7\).  
Therefore none of the \(\binom{9}{8}=9\) eight‑element subsets is
diverse.  
Thus a family of size \(9\) can avoid a diverse 8‑subset, so

\[
M>9 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 3.  Ten flags always contain a diverse eight‑subset  

Assume that a set \(F\) of ten distinct flags contains **no** diverse
8‑subset.  
Then for each colour \(c\) the graph \(G_c(F)\) has matching number at most
\(7\).  
By Kőnig’s theorem each of these graphs possesses a vertex cover of
size at most \(7\).

* For colour \(0\) let \(A\subset\{1,\dots ,8\}\) and \(X\subset F\) be a
minimum vertex cover, \(|A|+|X|\le 7\).  
All edges of colour \(0\) are incident with a vertex of the cover,
hence every flag not belonging to \(X\) has a **1** in every position of
\(A\). Consequently

\[
|F\setminus X|\le 10-(|A|+|X|)\le 10-7=3\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
|X|\ge 7-|A|.
\tag{1}
\]

* For colour \(1\) we obtain a similar pair \((B,Y)\) with
\(|B|+|Y|\le 7\) and every flag outside \(Y\) has a **0** in all positions
of \(B\). Hence

\[
|Y|\ge 7-|B|.
\tag{2}
\]

Because a flag cannot simultaneously have a 1 in a position of \(A\)
and a 0 in the same position of \(B\), the two sets \(F\setminus X\) and
\(F\setminus Y\) are disjoint. Therefore

\[
|F|\ge |F\setminus X|+|F\setminus Y|
      =\bigl(10-|X|\bigr)+\bigl(10-|Y|\bigr)
      \ge 20-(7-|A|)-(7-|B|)=6+|A|+|B|.
\]

Since \(|F|=10\), we obtain

\[
|A|+|B|\le 4. \tag{3}
\]

But from \(|A|+|X|\le7\) and \(|B|+|Y|\le7\) we have \(|A|,|B|\le 8\);
hence (3) forces \(|A|\le 4\) and \(|B|\le 4\).

Now consider the ten flags.  
At most \(|A|\le4\) of them can have a 0 in any of the positions of
\(A\); the remaining at least \(10-4=6\) flags have a 1 in **all**
positions of \(A\).  
Analogously, at most \(|B|\le4\) flags can have a 1 in a position of
\(B\); at least six flags have a 0 in all positions of \(B\).

Because \(|A|+|B|\le4\), the sets \(A\) and \(B\) are disjoint.
Consequently there are at least \(6+6=12\) distinct flags, contradicting
\(|F|=10\).

Hence our assumption was impossible: any ten flags **must** contain an
eight‑element subset whose graph \(G_c\) (for some colour \(c\)) has a
perfect matching, i.e. a diverse set.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 4.  Minimality  

We have exhibited a 9‑element family without a diverse 8‑subset and proved
that every 10‑element family contains a diverse 8‑subset.  
Therefore the smallest integer \(M\) with the required property is

\[
\boxed{M=10}.
\]

