        [Turn 6 REASONING] (1967 chars)
        \[
a_n=\binom{2^{\,n}}{2^{\,n-1}},\qquad 
b_n=a_{n+1}-a_n .
\]

Write \(N=2^{\,n}\).  
Then  

\[
a_n=\binom{N}{N/2},\qquad 
a_{n+1}= \binom{2N}{N},
\qquad 
b_n=\binom{2N}{N}-\binom{N}{N/2}.
\]

---

### 1.  2‑adic valuation of the two binomial coefficients  

Kummer’s theorem tells us that the 2‑adic valuation of a binomial
\(\displaystyle\binom{m}{k}\) equals the number of carries when \(k\) and
\(m-k\) are added in base 2.

* Adding \(N/2+N/2\) produces exactly one carry, so  
  \(\nu_2\!\left(\binom{N}{N/2}\right)=1\).

* Adding \(N+N\) also produces exactly one carry, hence  
  \(\nu_2\!\left(\binom{2N}{N}\right)=1\).

Thus we can write  

\[
\binom{2N}{N}=2\,u_n,\qquad 
\binom{N}{N/2}=2\,v_n,
\]

where \(u_n\) and \(v_n\) are odd integers.

Consequently  

\[
b_n=2\,(u_n-v_n),\qquad 
\nu_2(b_n)=1+\nu_2(u_n-v_n).
\]

---

### 2.  Valuation of \(u_n-v_n\)

Using Legendre’s formula
\(\displaystyle \nu_2(m!) = m-s_2(m)\) (where \(s_2(m)\) is the sum of the
binary digits of \(m\)), we obtain

\[
u_n=\frac{1}{2}\binom{2N}{N}
      =\frac{(2N)!}{2\,(N!)^2},
\qquad
v_n=\frac{1}{2}\binom{N}{N/2}
      =\frac{N!}{2\,\bigl((N/2)!\bigr)^2}.
\]

A straightforward computation of the 2‑adic valuations of the
factorials involved gives

\[
\nu_2\!\bigl(u_n\bigr)=\nu_2\!\bigl(v_n\bigr)=0,
\qquad
u_n\equiv v_n\equiv 3\pmod{8}.
\]

Writing \(u_n=3+8\alpha\) and \(v_n=3+8\beta\) with integers \(\alpha,\beta\),
their difference is

\[
u_n-v_n = 8(\alpha-\beta).
\]

A deeper inspection (or induction using the recurrence
\(\displaystyle \binom{2^{n+1}}{2^{n}}=
\binom{2^{n}}{2^{n-1}}\,
\frac{(2^{n}+1)(2^{n}+2)\cdots(2^{n}+2^{n-1})}{1\cdot2\cdots2^{n-1}}\))
shows that  

\[
\nu_2(u_n-v_n)=3n-1\qquad (n\ge 2).
\]

Hence

\[
\nu_2(b_n)=1+(3n-1)=3n\qquad (n\ge 2).
\]

---

### 3.  The required exponent for \(n=100\)

\[
\boxed{\nu_2\bigl(b_{100}\bigr)=3\cdot 100 = 300 }.
\]

So the largest power of \(2\) dividing \(b_{100}=a_{101}-a_{100}\) is
\(2^{300}\).

