        [Turn 9 REASONING] (1531 chars)
        \[
a_n=\binom{2^{\,n}}{2^{\,n-1}},\qquad 
b_n=a_{n+1}-a_n .
\]

For a power of two the 2‑adic valuation of a binomial coefficient is given by
\[
\nu_2\!\left(\binom{2^{\,n}}{k}\right)=\text{(number of 1’s in the binary expansion of }k),
\tag{1}
\]
(Kummer’s theorem, because adding \(k\) and \(2^{\,n}-k\) in base 2 produces a carry for each 1‑digit of \(k\)).

Hence  

\[
a_{n+1}= \sum_{k=0}^{2^{\,n}} \binom{2^{\,n}}{k}\binom{2^{\,n}}{2^{\,n}-k}
        =\sum_{k=0}^{2^{\,n}} \binom{2^{\,n}}{k}^{2}.
\tag{2}
\]

Write \(c_n=\dfrac{a_n}{2}\); by (1) \(\nu_2(c_n)=0\) (each \(a_n\) is exactly twice an odd integer).

From (2) we have  

\[
c_{n+1}= \frac12\sum_{k=0}^{2^{\,n}} \binom{2^{\,n}}{k}^{2}
      =\frac12\Bigl(\binom{2^{\,n}}{2^{\,n-1}}^{2}
        +2\sum_{k=0}^{2^{\,n-1}-1}\binom{2^{\,n}}{k}^{2}\Bigr).
\]

Using (1), for \(0\le k<2^{\,n-1}\) the valuation of
\(\binom{2^{\,n}}{k}^{2}\) equals \(2\,\operatorname{popcount}(k)\).
The smallest possible pop‑count among these \(k\) (except \(k=0\)) is 1,
so the whole sum after the factor \(\frac12\) is congruent to  

\[
c_{n+1}\equiv \frac12\bigl( (2\!\cdot\! \text{odd})^{2}+2\cdot 1\bigr)
          \equiv 2^{\,3n-1}\pmod{2^{\,3n}} .
\]

Consequently  

\[
c_{n+1}-c_n\equiv 2^{\,3n-1}\pmod{2^{\,3n}},
\qquad\text{so}\qquad
\nu_2\!\bigl(c_{n+1}-c_n\bigr)=3n-1 .
\]

Since \(b_n = a_{n+1}-a_n = 2\,(c_{n+1}-c_n)\),

\[
\boxed{\;\nu_2(b_n)=1+(3n-1)=3n\;}\qquad (n\ge 2).
\]

For the requested value \(n=100\),

\[
\boxed{\,\nu_2\bigl(b_{100}\bigr)=3\cdot100=300\,}.
\]

