        Let  

* \(B\) be the set of the black squares of the board,
* \(W\) the set of the white squares,
* \(G\) the knight‑graph of the board (vertices are the squares,
  two vertices are adjacent iff a knight can jump from one to the other).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  The size of a largest independent set  

A knight always moves from a black square to a white square, therefore
\(G\) is bipartite with the two colour classes \(B\) and \(W\).
Consequently all black squares (or all white squares) form an independent
set.  
\[
|B|=|W|=\frac{40\cdot 60}{2}=1200 .
\]

Hence the independence number of the graph is  

\[
\alpha(G)=1200 .\tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  What the opponent can do  

During the game each round consists of

* Horst puts a knight on an empty square that is **not adjacent** to any
  earlier knight (i.e. he adds a vertex to an independent set);
* Queenie puts a queen on an arbitrary empty square (the queen does not
  influence the legality of Horst’s later moves).

After \(t\) rounds exactly \(t\) squares are occupied by knights and
\(t\) squares are occupied by queens; altogether \(2t\) squares have been
removed from the board.

If Horst ever succeeds in placing more than \(\frac{\alpha(G)}{2}\)
knights, then more than \(\alpha(G)\) squares would have been removed
from the board (the \(t\) knights plus the \(t\) queens).  
But there are only \(\alpha(G)=1200\) squares that can belong to any
independent set, so after \(\frac{\alpha(G)}{2}=600\) rounds the board
contains at most the whole independent set and no further square can be
chosen without attacking an earlier knight.

Thus no strategy can guarantee more than  

\[
K_{\max}\le \frac{\alpha(G)}{2}=600 .\tag{2}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Horst can guarantee 600 knights  

Horst adopts the very simple strategy:

* always place his knight on a still empty black square.

All black squares are mutually non‑attacking, therefore every such move
is legal.  
Queenie can also only occupy one empty square per round; she can at most
remove one black square each round. After \(t\) rounds exactly \(t\) black
squares contain knights and \(t\) black squares contain queens, i.e. \(2t\)
black squares are no longer available.

Since there are \(1200\) black squares, the game must end no later than
when \(2t=1200\); that is, after \(t=600\) rounds.  At that moment Horst
has placed **600** knights, and the game is finished because no empty
black square remains; any empty white square would be attacked by at
least one of the already placed knights (the set of 600 black knights is a
vertex cover of the bipartite knight‑graph).

Hence Horst can **always** achieve at least 600 knights, no matter how
Queenie plays.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Maximal guaranteed number  

From (2) we have an upper bound \(K_{\max}\le 600\); from the strategy
above we have a lower bound \(K_{\max}\ge 600\).  Therefore the bound is
tight.

\[
\boxed{K_{\max}=600}
\]

So, regardless of Queenie’s choices, Horst can guarantee that at least
\(600\) knights will be placed on the \(40\times 60\) board.

