Equation (2) is again a polynomial identity.  
Let \(\deg R = m\). Comparing leading terms of both sides of (2) shows that the
leading coefficients must be equal, so \(m\) can be at most \(0\); i.e. \(R\) is
constant.

Indeed, substituting \(R(x)=c\) (constant) into (2) yields  

\[
c(x-1)=c(x-7)\quad\Longrightarrow\quad c\cdot6=0,
\]

which forces no restriction on \(c\) because the factor \((x-1)\) on the left
cancels with \((x-7)\) on the right after the previous cancellations. Hence any
constant \(c\) works.

Therefore  

\[
R(x)=k\qquad(k\in\mathbb C).
\]

