\Bigl(1-\frac{3}{x}\Bigr)^{d}
=
\Bigl(1-\frac{4}{x}\Bigr)\Bigl(1-\frac{7}{x}\Bigr).
\]

Expanding the first order terms in \(1/x\) gives  

\[
1-\frac{3d}{x}+O\!\left(\frac1{x^{2}}\right)
=
1-\frac{12}{x}+O\!\left(\frac1{x^{2}}\right),
\]

so \(-3d=-12\Rightarrow d=4\).

Thus every non‑zero solution is a quartic polynomial.

