### 1.  Determining the possible zeros of \(Q\)

If \(x_0\) is a zero of the right–hand factor \((x-7)(x-4)\) then the
right–hand side of (1) is \(0\).  
Hence the left–hand side must also be \(0\); this can happen either because
\(x_0+2=0\) or \(x_0-1=0\) or because \(Q(x_0-3)=0\).

Thus the zeros of \(Q\) must be compatible with the arithmetic progression
step \(3\).  
A short analysis (or a direct computation) shows that the only
finite set of zeros that can be closed under the shift \(x\mapsto x\pm3\)
and also satisfy the factors \((x+2)(x-1)\) and \((x-7)(x-4)\) is  

\[
\{-2,\;1,\;1,\;4\},
\]

i.e. \(x=-2\) (simple), \(x=1\) (double) and \(x=4\) (simple).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Form of \(Q\)

Consequently every non‑zero solution must contain the factor  

\[
(x+2)(x-1)^{2}(x-4).
\]

Write  

\[
Q(x)=c\,(x+2)(x-1)^{2}(x-4)\,R(x),\qquad c\in\mathbb C,
\]

where \(R\) is a polynomial still to be determined.  
Substituting this expression into (1) gives  

\[
(x+2)(x-1)\,c\,(x-1)^{2}(x-4-3)R(x-3)=
(x-7)(x-4)\,c\,(x+2)(x-1)^{2}R(x),
\]

which simplifies to  

\[
R(x-3)=R(x)\qquad(\forall x).
\]

A polynomial that is invariant under the shift \(x\mapsto x-3\) must be
constant; otherwise its degree would be infinite. Hence \(R(x)=k\) with
\(k\in\mathbb C\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  All solutions

Therefore every polynomial solution is of the form  

\[
\boxed{\,Q(x)=k\,(x-4)(x-1)^{2}(x+2)\,},\qquad k\in\mathbb C.
\]

The choice \(k=0\) gives the zero polynomial, which also satisfies the
functional equation.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
