### 1.  Roots of \(Q\)

If \(a\) is a root of \(Q\) and \(a\neq1,-2\) then from (1)  

\[
0= B(a)Q(a)=A(a)Q(a-3)\Longrightarrow Q(a-3)=0 .
\]

Hence any root not equal to \(1\) or \(-2\) forces the root \(a-3\) as well.
Repeating, we must eventually hit a root of \(A\); otherwise we would obtain infinitely many
distinct roots, impossible for a polynomial.  
Thus every root of \(Q\) is of the form  

\[
a=1+3k\quad\text{or}\quad a=-2+3k\qquad(k\in\mathbb Z_{\ge 0}).
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Multiplicity recurrence

Let \(m(r)\) be the multiplicity of the root \(r\) in \(Q\).
From (1),

\[
m(r-3)+\mathbf 1_{r\in\{1,-2\}}=m(r)+\mathbf 1_{r\in\{4,7\}},
\tag{2}
\]

where \(\mathbf 1_{S}\) is \(1\) if the condition holds and \(0\) otherwise.
Only the numbers \(1,-2,4,7\) appear in (2); they all belong to the same
congruence class \(r\equiv1\pmod 3\).
For the other two residue classes the right‑hand side of (2) is zero,
so the multiplicities there are constant along the class; finiteness forces them to be \(0\).
Hence all roots lie in the class \(1\pmod 3\).

