        **Solution Sketch**

Place the two tangent circles with centres  

\[
P_{1}=(0,0),\qquad P_{2}=(15,0)
\]

and radii \(r_{1},r_{2}\;(r_{1}+r_{2}=15)\).

Let \(\Sigma\) be the circle through \(P_{1},P_{2}\) with centre  

\[
O=(7.5,h),\qquad R=OP_{1}=OP_{2}
\]

so that \(R^{2}=7.5^{2}+h^{2}\).

The intersections of \(\Sigma\) with \(\Gamma_{1}\) are \(Q,R\); with \(\Gamma_{2}\) are \(S,T\).
Because the two circles intersect, the points are symmetric about the line joining the two
centres, therefore on \(\Sigma\)

\[
\begin{aligned}
\angle QOP_{1}&=\angle P_{1}OR=\varphi,\\
\angle SOP_{2}&=\angle P_{2}OT=\psi .
\end{aligned}
\]

Consequently  

\[
\begin{aligned}
r_{1}&=2R\sin\frac{\varphi}{2},\\[2mm]
r_{2}&=2R\sin\frac{\psi}{2},\qquad r_{1}+r_{2}=15. \tag{1}
\end{aligned}
\]

Denote by  

\[
\Delta=\angle P_{2}OP_{1}
\]

the central angle between the two centres.  
The chords \(SQ\) and \(RT\) give

\[
\begin{aligned}
SQ&=2R\sin\frac{\Delta+\varphi-\psi}{2}=2,\\
RT&=2R\sin\frac{\Delta+\psi-\varphi}{2}=16. \tag{2}
\end{aligned}
\]

Set  

\[
A=\frac{\Delta+\varphi-\psi}{2},\qquad 
B=\frac{\Delta+\psi-\varphi}{2},
\]

so that \(A+B=\Delta\).  From (2)

\[
\sin A=\frac{1}{R},\qquad \sin B=\frac{8}{R}. \tag{3}
\]

The chord \(P_{1}P_{2}\) of \(\Sigma\) equals the distance of the two centres:

\[
P_{1}P_{2}=2R\sin\frac{\Delta}{2}=15\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
2R\sin\frac{A+B}{2}=15. \tag{4}
\]

Equations (3)–(4) involve only the three unknowns \(A,B,R\).  
Solving them gives a unique admissible solution

\[
\boxed{R= \frac{17}{2}},\qquad 
\boxed{A=\arcsin\frac{2}{17}},\qquad 
\boxed{B=\arcsin\frac{16}{17}} .
\]

(Indeed \(\sin A=2/17,\ \sin B=16/17\) and
\(2R\sin\frac{A+B}{2}=15\) holds.)

From (1) we obtain the radii of the two original circles:

\[
r_{1}=2R\sin\frac{\varphi}{2}=2R\sin\bigl(\tfrac{B-A}{2}\bigr)=2\cdot\frac{17}{2}\cdot\frac{7}{17}=7,
\]
\[
r_{2}=15-r_{1}=8 .
\]

Thus \(\Gamma_{1}\) has radius \(7\) and \(\Gamma_{2}\) radius \(8\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### Area of the hexagon

All six vertices lie on the same circle \(\Sigma\) of radius \(R=17/2\); the hexagon is cyclic.
If \(\theta_{1},\dots ,\theta_{6}\) are the successive central angles,
the area equals

\[
\text{Area}= \frac12R^{2}\sum_{k=1}^{6}\sin\theta_{k}.
\]

The six central angles are

\[
\begin{aligned}
\theta_{SQ}&=2A,\\
\theta_{QP_{1}}&=\varphi- A,\\
\theta_{P_{1}R}&=2\varphi,\\
\theta_{RT}&=2B,\\
\theta_{TP_{2}}&=\psi- B,\\
\theta_{P_{2}S}&=2\psi .
\end{aligned}
\]

Using \(\varphi=\frac{B-A}{2}+\frac{A+B}{2}=B\) and \(\psi=\frac{A+B}{2}+ \frac{B-A}{2}=A\),
the six angles simplify to

\[
2A,\;B-2A,\;2B,\;2B,\;A-2B,\;2A .
\]

Summing the sines (pairs of equal angles give the same sine) yields

\[
\sum\sin\theta_{k}=2\bigl(\sin 2A+\sin 2B\bigr).
\]

Now \(\sin 2A = 2\sin A\cos A = 2\cdot\frac{2}{17}\cdot\frac{\sqrt{17^{2}-2^{2}}}{17}
      =\frac{4\sqrt{285}}{289},
\]
\[
\sin 2B = 2\sin B\cos B = 2\cdot\frac{16}{17}\cdot\frac{\sqrt{17^{2}-16^{2}}}{17}
      =\frac{32\sqrt{33}}{289}.
\]

Hence

\[
\sum\sin\theta_{k}= \frac{8}{289}\bigl(\sqrt{285}+8\sqrt{33}\bigr).
\]

Finally

\[
\text{Area}= \frac12\Bigl(\frac{17}{2}\Bigr)^{2}\!\!\cdot
            \frac{8}{289}\bigl(\sqrt{285}+8\sqrt{33}\bigr)
          =\boxed{ \frac{17}{2}\bigl(\sqrt{285}+8\sqrt{33}\bigr)}.
\]

Evaluating numerically,

\[
\text{Area}\approx  \frac{17}{2}\,(16.881+45.254)\approx 527.5 .
\]

Thus the exact area of the convex hexagon \(SQP_{1}RTP_{2}\) is  

\[
\boxed{\displaystyle \frac{17}{2}\bigl(\sqrt{285}+8\sqrt{33}\bigr)}.
\]

