--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 1.  What the condition means  

Let  

\[
D(m)=\{d_{1},d_{2},\dots ,d_{n}\}
\qquad (n=\tau (m))
\]

be the set of all positive divisors of \(m\) written in some order.
Define  

\[
s_{i}=d_{1}+d_{2}+ \cdots +d_{i}\qquad (1\le i\le n).
\]

The hypothesis is  

\[
s_{i}\ \text{is a perfect square for every }i .
\tag{1}
\]

Consequences of (1) that we shall use repeatedly are  

* \(s_{i}\equiv 0,1\pmod 4\) (squares are \(0\) or \(1\) mod 4);
* the difference of two squares is never \(\equiv 2\pmod 4\); hence  

  \[
  d_{i}=s_{i}-s_{i-1}\not\equiv 2\pmod 4\qquad (i\ge 2).
  \tag{2}
  \]

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 2.  \(m\) cannot be even  

If \(m\) is even, \(2\in D(m)\).  
From (2) a divisor congruent to \(2\pmod 4\) can never appear, a contradiction.  
Thus **\(m\) must be odd**.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 3.  The first two terms  

Because \(1\) is a divisor of every integer and \(1=1^{2}\) is a square,
the only possible first term is  

\[
d_{1}=1,\qquad s_{1}=1 .
\tag{3}
\]

Let \(s_{2}=t^{2}\;(t\ge 2)\).  Then  

\[
d_{2}=s_{2}-s_{1}=t^{2}-1=(t-1)(t+1).
\tag{4}
\]

If \(t\) were odd, both factors in (4) would be even, whence
\(d_{2}\) would be even – impossible because \(m\) is odd.
Hence **\(t\) is even**, say \(t=2k\).  Substituting in (4) gives  

\[
d_{2}= (2k-1)(2k+1)=4k^{2}-1\equiv 3\pmod 4 .
\tag{5}
\]

Thus the second divisor is congruent to \(3\pmod 4\); in particular
\(m\) possesses a prime divisor \(p\equiv 3\pmod 4\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 4.  The smallest prime divisor of \(m\)  

Let \(p\) be the smallest prime divisor of \(m\).  
From (5) we have \(p\mid d_{2}\); consequently \(p\mid (t-1)(t+1)\).
Since \(p\) is odd, \(t\equiv \pm 1\pmod p\) and therefore  

\[
t^{2}\equiv 1\pmod p .
\tag{6}
\]

Because \(s_{2}=t^{2}\) and \(s_{1}=1\), (6) shows that the two
initial square‑partial sums are congruent to \(1\) modulo \(p\).

Now consider any later partial sum \(s_{i}=u^{2}\) \((i\ge 2)\).
If a divisor not divisible by \(p\) were added at step \(i\),
the congruence of the new square modulo \(p\) would change,
contradicting (6).  Hence **every divisor of \(m\) is a multiple of \(p\)**,
i.e. \(m=p^{\alpha}\) for some \(\alpha\ge 1\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 5.  The exponent \(\alpha\)  

Write \(m=p^{\alpha}\;(p\equiv 3\pmod 4)\).
All its divisors are the powers \(1,p,p^{2},\dots ,p^{\alpha}\).
Because the first term must be \(1\) and the second term must be
\(d_{2}=p\) (the only divisor larger than \(1\) that is a multiple of \(p\)),
(4) forces  

\[
p=t^{2}-1\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad p=(t-1)(t+1).
\]

Since \(p\) is prime, one of the factors equals \(1\); thus \(t-1=1\) and
\(t=2\).  Consequently \(p=3\).

So the only possible prime divisor is \(p=3\) and the only admissible
power is \(\alpha=1\); otherwise the second divisor would be
\(3^{2}=9\neq t^{2}-1\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 6.  Verification for \(m=3\)

The divisors of \(3\) are \(\{1,3\}\).
The ordering  

\[
(1,3) \qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
s_{1}=1=1^{2},\; s_{2}=1+3=4=2^{2}
\]

satisfies (1).  Hence \(m=3\) indeed works.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 7.  No other integer works  

We have shown:

* \(m\) must be odd;
* its smallest prime divisor must be \(3\);
* the only possible power of \(3\) is \(3^{1}\).

Thus the unique integer \(m>1\) for which a permutation of its
divisors can have all prefix sums perfect squares is \(m=3\).

