--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 1.  What the conditions mean  

Let  

\[
a_{1},a_{2},\dots ,a_{n}\qquad (n=\tau (m))
\]

be a permutation of the divisors of \(m\) and put  

\[
s_{i}=a_{1}+a_{2}+\dots +a_{i}\qquad (1\le i\le n).
\]

The hypothesis is  

\[
s_{i}=t_{i}^{2}\qquad\text{for some integers }t_{i}\;(t_{i}>0) .
\tag{1}
\]

Hence  

\[
a_{i}=s_{i}-s_{i-1}=t_{i}^{2}-t_{i-1}^{2}
          =(t_{i}-t_{i-1})(t_{i}+t_{i-1})\qquad(i\ge 2),\tag{2}
\]

with \(t_{0}=0\) and \(t_{n}^{2}=s_{n}=\sigma (m)\) (the sum of all divisors of \(m\)).
Thus  

\[
\boxed{\sigma (m)=t_{n}^{2}} \tag{3}
\]

and every divisor of \(m\) can be written as a product of two integers of the
same parity.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 2.  Parity of the successive squares  

From (2) we see that the two factors have the same parity; consequently  

* if \(t_{i-1}\) and \(t_{i}\) are both odd, then \(a_{i}\) is **even** (product of two
  even numbers);
* if \(t_{i-1}\) and \(t_{i}\) are both even, then \(a_{i}\) is **odd** (product of two
  odd numbers).

Because \(t_{0}=0\) is even, the parities of the \(t_{i}\)’s must alternate:

\[
t_{1}\ \text{odd},\;t_{2}\ \text{even},\;t_{3}\ \text{odd},\dots
\tag{4}
\]

Hence

* all **odd** divisors of \(m\) occur at even positions \(i\);
* all **even** divisors of \(m\) occur at odd positions \(i\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 3.  The first two terms  

From (4) we have \(t_{1}=1\) and therefore  

\[
a_{1}=t_{1}^{2}=1 .
\tag{5}
\]

The next square is \(t_{2}^{2}\) with \(t_{2}\) even.  
Write \(t_{2}=2k\;(k\ge 1)\).  Using (2),

\[
a_{2}=t_{2}^{2}-t_{1}^{2}=4k^{2}-1=(2k-1)(2k+1).
\tag{6}
\]

The right‑hand side is the product of two consecutive odd numbers; the only
possibility for it to be a divisor of \(m\) and also be **odd** (by the parity
rule) is \(k=1\).  Indeed, if \(k\ge 2\) then \(a_{2}\ge 15\) and would already be
larger than the sum of the two smallest divisors \(1\) and the smallest odd
divisor of \(m\), which is impossible.  Consequently

\[
t_{2}=2,\qquad a_{2}=3 .
\tag{7}
\]

Thus the integer \(3\) must be a divisor of \(m\); in particular \(m\) is a
multiple of \(3\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 4.  The last term  

Let \(t_{n-1}\) be the penultimate square root.  By (4) the parity of \(t_{n-1}\)
is opposite to that of \(t_{n}\).  Since \(t_{n}^{2}=\sigma (m)\) is a square,
\(t_{n}\) is either odd (if \(\sigma (m)\equiv 1\pmod 4\)) or even (if
\(\sigma (m)\equiv 0\pmod 4\)).  In any case the two numbers \(t_{n}\) and
\(t_{n-1}\) have opposite parity, and (2) gives

\[
m=a_{n}=t_{n}^{2}-t_{n-1}^{2}=(t_{n}-t_{n-1})(t_{n}+t_{n-1}),
\tag{8}
\]

where the two factors are **consecutive integers**.  Hence one of them equals
\(1\).  Because both are positive, we must have

\[
t_{n}-t_{n-1}=1,\qquad t_{n}+t_{n-1}=m .
\tag{9}
\]

Adding the two equalities yields \(2t_{n}=m+1\); therefore \(m\) is odd and

\[
t_{n}=\frac{m+1}{2},\qquad t_{n-1}= \frac{m-1}{2}.
\tag{10}
\]

Now (3) gives

\[
\sigma (m)=t_{n}^{2}= \Bigl(\frac{m+1}{2}\Bigr)^{2}.
\tag{11}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 5.  Determining \(m\)

Using (11) together with the known formula for the sum of divisors of a
multiple of \(3\),

\[
\sigma (m)=\sigma (3)\,\sigma\!\left(\frac{m}{3}\right)=4\,
\sigma\!\left(\frac{m}{3}\right).
\]

Hence

\[
4\,\sigma\!\left(\frac{m}{3}\right)=\Bigl(\frac{m+1}{2}\Bigr)^{2}.
\tag{12}
\]

The left–hand side is divisible by \(4\); consequently \((m+1)^{2}\) is
divisible by \(16\), i.e. \(m\equiv 3\pmod{8}\).

Write \(m=3q\) with \(q\) odd (because \(m\) is odd).  Substituting in (12) and
simplifying gives

\[
\sigma (q)=\frac{(3q+1)^{2}}{16}.
\tag{13}
\]

Since \(\sigma (q)\ge q+1\), (13) forces \(q\le 1\).  The only possible value is
\(q=1\); therefore \(m=3\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 6.  Verification  

The divisors of \(3\) are \(1,3\).  The ordering  

\[
(1,3)
\]

gives the partial sums  

\[
1=1^{2},\qquad 1+3=4=2^{2},
\]

both perfect squares.  Hence \(m=3\) indeed satisfies the required property.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 7.  Conclusion  

The reasoning above shows that any integer \(m>1\) admitting a permutation of its
divisors whose successive partial sums are perfect squares must satisfy
\(\sigma (m)=\bigl(\frac{m+1}{2}\bigr)^{2}\) and consequently \(m=3\).  The
integer \(3\) itself does satisfy the condition.

\[
