        \[
\boxed{m=3}
\]

**Proof.**  
Let  

\[
a_{1},a_{2},\dots ,a_{n}\qquad (n=\tau (m))
\]

be a permutation of the positive divisors of \(m\) such that  

\[
S_{i}=a_{1}+a_{2}+\dots +a_{i}=t_{i}^{2}\qquad (1\le i\le n)
\]

is a perfect square for every \(i\).  
Put \(t_{0}=0\); then  

\[
a_{i}=S_{i}-S_{i-1}=t_{i}^{2}-t_{i-1}^{2}
          =(t_{i}-t_{i-1})(t_{i}+t_{i-1})\tag{1}
\]

so each divisor of \(m\) is a product of two integers of the **same
parity** (the two factors in (1) have the same parity).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  The number \(2\) cannot occur

If an even divisor occurs, both factors in (1) are even, hence the
divisor is a multiple of \(4\).  
Consequently a divisor congruent to \(2\pmod 4\) cannot be present.
If \(m\) were even, the divisor \(2\) would appear – a contradiction.
Thus **\(m\) is odd**.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  The smallest divisor larger than \(1\) must be \(3\)

The smallest divisor larger than \(1\) is the smallest prime divisor of
\(m\); denote it by \(p\).  
From (1) we have  

\[
p=(t_{k}-t_{k-1})(t_{k}+t_{k-1})
\]

with \(t_{k}\) and \(t_{k-1}\) of opposite parity (otherwise the product
would be even).  
Hence both factors are odd.  
Since \(p\) is prime, one of the factors must be \(1\); therefore  

\[
t_{k}-t_{k-1}=1,\qquad p=2t_{k-1}+1 .
\]

The first square is \(t_{1}^{2}=a_{1}=1\); thus \(t_{1}=1\) and
\(t_{2}=2\).  Consequently  

\[
p=2\cdot 1+1=3 .
\]

So the smallest prime divisor of \(m\) is \(3\); in particular \(3\mid m\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  No other divisor can exist

Assume that besides \(1\) and \(3\) the set of divisors contains a
number \(d>3\) and let \(i\) be the index with \(a_{i}=d\).
From (1) and the parity discussion we have \(t_{i-1}\ge 2\) and

\[
d=(t_{i}-t_{i-1})(t_{i}+t_{i-1})\ge 2t_{i-1}+1\ge5 .\tag{2}
\]

If the equality in (2) holds then \(t_{i}=t_{i-1}+1\); otherwise
\(t_{i}>t_{i-1}+1\).  In either case

\[
t_{i}\ge t_{i-1}+1\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
a_{i+1}=t_{i+1}^{2}-t_{i}^{2}\ge 2t_{i}+1\ge d+2 .\tag{3}
\]

Thus after the appearance of a divisor \(d>3\) every later divisor is at
least \(2\) larger than the preceding one.  Consequently the set of
divisors of \(m\) would contain an arithmetic progression of odd numbers

\[
d,\;d+2,\;d+4,\dots ,\le m .
\]

If such a progression contains two consecutive odd numbers, say
\(k\) and \(k+2\), then both divide \(m\); hence their product
\(k(k+2)\) also divides \(m\).  Repeating this argument shows that the
product of all numbers in the progression divides \(m\), which quickly
exceeds \(m\) itself – an impossibility.  Therefore the progression can
contain **no** term larger than \(3\).

Hence the only possible divisors are \(1\) and \(3\); i.e. \(\tau(m)=2\)
and \(m\) is a prime.  Because the smallest prime divisor must be \(3\),
the only admissible integer is

\[
m=3 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Verification for \(m=3\)

The divisors of \(3\) are \(1,3\).  Taking the order \((1,3)\),

\[
1=1^{2},\qquad 1+3=4=2^{2},
\]

so the required property holds.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Since every other integer \(m>1\) leads to a contradiction, the unique
solution is  

\[
\boxed{m=3}.
\]

