        \[
\text{Find all }m>1\text{ for which the divisors of }m\text{ can be ordered }
(a_{1},a_{2},\dots ,a_{n})
\]
\[
\text{so that }S_{i}=a_{1}+a_{2}+\dots +a_{i}\;(1\le i\le n)
\text{ is a perfect square.}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  First consequences

*Let \(d\) be any divisor of \(m\).  
Because \(d=S_{i}-S_{i-1}\) is the difference of two squares,
\(d\) is either odd or a multiple of \(4\) (difference of two squares
\((x-y)(x+y)\) has the same parity for the two factors).  

Hence a divisor congruent to \(2\pmod 4\) can never occur.
If \(m\) were even, the divisor \(2\mid m\) would be \(2\bmod 4\);
therefore **\(m\) must be odd** and all its divisors are odd.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  The first term

Since the smallest divisor of an odd integer is \(1\), the first term
must be  

\[
a_{1}=1,\qquad S_{1}=1=1^{2}.
\]

(If \(a_{1}>1\) then \(a_{1}\) itself would be an odd square,
hence a proper square divisor of \(m\); consequently \(m\) would have
at least three distinct divisors, and we shall see that a chain of
length \(\ge 3\) is impossible – see the next section.)

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  How the next terms look

Write the successive squares as  

\[
S_{i}=x_{i}^{2}\qquad (x_{1}=1<x_{2}<x_{3}<\dots ).
\]

Then  

\[
a_{i}=S_{i}-S_{i-1}=x_{i}^{2}-x_{i-1}^{2}=(x_{i}-x_{i-1})(x_{i}+x_{i-1}).
\]

Because every \(a_{i}\) is odd, the two factors have the same parity.
Consequently the parity of the \(x_{i}\)’s alternates:
\(x_{1}=1\) is odd, so \(x_{2}\) is even, \(x_{3}\) odd, etc.
Hence the number of divisors \(n\) (the length of the chain) is odd,
and the final square \(S_{n}=\sigma(m)\) (the sum of all divisors) is
odd.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Minimal possible values

From the parity pattern we obtain the smallest possible values of the
differences.

* \(x_{2}=2\) gives  
  \[
  a_{2}=x_{2}^{2}-x_{1}^{2}=4-1=3 .
  \]

* \(x_{3}=3\) (the smallest odd number larger than \(x_{2}=2\)) gives  
  \[
  a_{3}=x_{3}^{2}-x_{2}^{2}=9-4=5 .
  \]

* The next even square is \(x_{4}=4\); then  
  \[
  a_{4}=x_{4}^{2}-x_{3}^{2}=16-9=7\quad\text{(not a difference of two
  squares of the same parity)} .
  \]
  The smallest *admissible* even \(x_{4}\) is \(x_{4}=8\); then  

  \[
  a_{4}=8^{2}-3^{2}=64-9=55 .
  \]

Thus, if the chain contains at least four terms we must have  

\[
\{a_{1},a_{2},a_{3},a_{4}\}\supset\{1,3,5,55\}.
\tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Impossibility of a chain of length \(\ge 3\)

Assume that the chain has at least three terms.
Then the three smallest terms are forced to be \(1,3,5\) (the values
found above).  
Consequently the integer \(m\) is divisible by \(3\) and by \(5\); hence
\(15\mid m\).

But \(15\) itself is a divisor of \(m\), and it must appear somewhere in
the list \((a_{i})\).  
From (1) we see that the fourth term, if it exists, is at least \(55\);
there is **no place** for the divisor \(15\) among the required
differences, because every difference of two squares with the same
parity is either \(1,3,5\) or at least \(55\).  
Thus a chain of length \(\ge 3\) cannot be realised.

Hence the only possible length is  

\[
n=2 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 6.  The case \(n=2\)

If \(n=2\) the divisor set of \(m\) is \(\{1,p\}\); therefore \(m\) is
prime.
The conditions are

\[
a_{1}=1,\qquad a_{2}=p,\qquad 1+p\text{ is a perfect square.}
\]

Write \(1+p=k^{2}\;(k>1)\).  Because \(p\) is prime, one of the factors
\(k-1\) and \(k+1\) must be \(1\).  The only possibility is \(k-1=1\),
i.e. \(k=2\).  Hence \(p=3\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 7.  Verification

For \(m=3\) the divisors are \(1,3\) and  

\[
(1,3):\quad 1=1^{2},\;1+3=4=2^{2}.
\]

Thus the required ordering exists.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 8.  Answer

\[
\boxed{\,m=3\,}
\]

No other integer \(m>1\) admits a permutation of its divisors whose
partial sums are all perfect squares.

