--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 1.  What the condition means  

Let  

\[
D(m)=\{d_{1},d_{2},\dots ,d_{n}\}\qquad (n=\tau (m))
\]

be the set of all positive divisors of \(m\) (written in some order).  
Put  

\[
S_{k}=d_{1}+d_{2}+\dots +d_{k}\qquad (k=1,\dots ,n).
\]

The hypothesis is  

\[
S_{k}=t_{k}^{2}\qquad (t_{k}\in \mathbb Z_{>0})\tag{1}
\]

for every \(k\).  
Consequently  

* \(S_{n}= \sigma (m)=t_{n}^{2}\) – the sum of all divisors must be a perfect
  square;
* \(d_{k}=S_{k}-S_{k-1}=t_{k}^{2}-t_{k-1}^{2}=(t_{k}-t_{k-1})(t_{k}+t_{k-1})\).

Thus every divisor of \(m\) is a product of two integers of the **same
parity**.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 2.  The parity of the divisors  

If a divisor were even but not a multiple of \(4\) (i.e. \( \equiv 2\pmod 4\)),
its two factors could not have the same parity – one would be even,
the other odd.  
Hence an admissible \(m\) cannot contain such a divisor; consequently  

\[
\boxed{m\text{ is odd}} .\tag{2}
\]

All divisors of an odd integer are odd, and an odd number is always a
product of two odd numbers, so (2) satisfies the parity condition.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 3.  Parity of the successive squares  

Because all differences \(d_{k}\) are odd, the squares in (1) must
alternate parity:

\[
0=S_{0}\equiv 0\pmod 2,\;S_{1}\equiv 1,\;S_{2}\equiv 0,\;S_{3}\equiv 1,\dots
\]

Hence  

\[
S_{k}\equiv 
\begin{cases}
1\pmod 2 & k\text{ odd},\\[2mm]
0\pmod 2 & k\text{ even}.
\end{cases}
\tag{3}
\]

Since every divisor is odd, the number of divisors
\(\tau(m)=n\) has the same parity as \(\sigma(m)=S_{n}\).  
For an odd integer \(m\),

\[
\sigma(m)\equiv \tau(m)\pmod 2 .
\]

Therefore

* if \(m\) is **not** a perfect square, \(\tau(m)\) is even, \(n\) is even and
  \(\sigma(m)\) is even;
* if \(m\) **is** a perfect square, \(\tau(m)\) is odd, \(n\) is odd and
  \(\sigma(m)\) is odd.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 4.  The case \(\boldsymbol{n=2}\)  

\(n=2\) occurs exactly when \(m\) is prime.  
Then the only possible order is \((1,m)\) and (1) gives  

\[
1=m_{1}^{2},\qquad 1+m=m_{2}^{2}.
\]

Thus \(m+1\) must be a square.  Write \(m+1=(2k)^{2}=4k^{2}\); then  

\[
m=4k^{2}-1=(2k-1)(2k+1).
\]

Because \(m\) is prime, one of the factors equals \(1\); hence \(2k-1=1\),
\(k=1\) and \(m=3\).

So the prime case yields the single solution  

\[
\boxed{m=3}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 5.  The case \(\boldsymbol{n\ge 3}\) – impossibility  

Assume now that \(n\ge 3\).  
From (3) we have  

\[
S_{1}=1,\qquad S_{2}=t_{2}^{2}\;(t_{2}\text{ even}),\qquad 
S_{3}=t_{3}^{2}\;(t_{3}\text{ odd}).
\]

Set \(t_{2}=2u\;(u\ge 1)\).  
Then  

\[
d_{2}=S_{2}-S_{1}=4u^{2}-1=(2u-1)(2u+1). \tag{4}
\]

Both factors in (4) are odd, so \(d_{2}\) is the product of two
consecutive odd numbers.

Now \(t_{3}\) is odd; write \(t_{3}=2v+1\;(v\ge u)\).  Using
\(S_{3}=t_{3}^{2}=4v^{2}+4v+1\),

\[
d_{3}=S_{3}-S_{2}=4v^{2}+4v+1-4u^{2}
   =4(v^{2}+v-u^{2})+1. \tag{5}
\]

The right–hand side of (5) is **congruent to \(1\pmod 4\)**.
Consequently every divisor that appears after the second step must be
\(\equiv 1\pmod 4\).

But (4) shows that the second divisor \(d_{2}=4u^{2}-1\) is
\(\equiv 3\pmod 4\) (because \(4u^{2}\equiv 0\pmod 4\)).  
Thus among the divisors of \(m\) we would need **both** a number
\(\equiv 3\pmod 4\) (the second one) and a number \(\equiv 1\pmod 4\)
(the third one).  

All odd divisors of an odd integer are either all \(\equiv 1\pmod 4\) or
all \(\equiv 3\pmod 4\) **iff** the integer is a perfect square.
Indeed, if \(m\) is a square, every divisor occurs in a complementary
pair \(d\) and \(m/d\); one of the pair is \(\equiv 1\pmod 4\) and the
other \(\equiv 3\pmod 4\).  Hence a square has divisors of **both**
congruence classes, while a non‑square has divisors of only one class.

*If \(m\) is not a square*, all its divisors are congruent to the same
odd residue modulo \(4\); then (4) and (5) cannot both be satisfied.
Thus no ordering exists when \(m\) is non‑square and \(n\ge 3\).

*If \(m\) is a square*, the number of divisors \(n\) is odd, so the
final square \(S_{n}\) is odd.  Yet the last step adds the largest
divisor \(m\) to an even square (the preceding one), which would give
an **even** final sum – a contradiction.  Hence a square \(m\) also
cannot admit an ordering with \(n\ge 3\).

Therefore **no integer \(m>1\) with \(\tau(m)\ge 3\) satisfies the required
property**.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 6.  Conclusion  

The only admissible integer is the one coming from the case \(n=2\),
namely \(m=3\).  For this \(m\) the ordering  

\[
(1,3)
\]

gives the partial sums  

\[
1=1^{2},\qquad 1+3=4=2^{2},
\]

which indeed are perfect squares.

