        \[
\text{Find all }m>1\text{ for which the divisors of }m\text{ can be ordered }
(a_{1},a_{2},\dots ,a_{n})
\]
\[
\text{so that }S_i=a_{1}+a_{2}+\cdots +a_{i}\text{ is a perfect square for every }i .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  What the condition means  

Put  

\[
S_{0}=0,\qquad S_i=s_i^{\,2}\;(s_i\in \mathbb Z_{>0}),\qquad 
a_i=S_i-S_{i-1}=s_i^{2}-s_{i-1}^{2}.
\]

Hence each divisor of \(m\) is a **difference of two squares**  

\[
a_i=(s_i-s_{i-1})(s_i+s_{i-1}) .
\tag{1}
\]

The two factors in (1) have the **same parity**; consequently

* an odd divisor can be written as a product of two odd numbers,
* an even divisor can be written only as a product of two even numbers,
  i.e. it must be a multiple of \(4\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  No even divisor  

If \(m\) were even, the divisor \(2\) would have to appear in the
sequence, but \(2\) is not a multiple of \(4\).  
Thus **\(m\) must be odd** and all its divisors are odd.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  The first two gaps  

The first sum is \(S_{1}=a_{1}=s_{1}^{2}\).  
Since the smallest divisor is \(1\), we must have \(a_{1}=1\) and
\(s_{1}=1\).

The second sum is \(S_{2}=1+a_{2}=s_{2}^{2}\); therefore  

\[
a_{2}=s_{2}^{2}-1=(s_{2}-1)(s_{2}+1).
\tag{2}
\]

Because \(a_{2}\) is odd, the two factors in (2) are odd; hence
\(s_{2}\) is even, say \(s_{2}=2k\).  Then  

\[
a_{2}= (2k-1)(2k+1)=4k^{2}-1\equiv 3\pmod 4 .
\tag{3}
\]

Consequently the second divisor must be congruent to \(3\pmod 4\) and
must be of the special form \(k^{2}-1\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  The smallest possible second divisor  

Let \(p\) be the smallest divisor of \(m\) larger than \(1\).  
From (3) we have \(p=k^{2}-1=(k-1)(k+1)\).
If \(p\) is prime, one of the factors must be \(1\); thus \(k-1=1\)
and \(k=2\).  Hence  

\[
p=2^{2}-1=3 .
\]

So the smallest divisor \(>1\) has to be \(3\); therefore \(3\mid m\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  What happens after the first two gaps  

After the first two gaps we have  

\[
S_{2}=1+3=4=2^{2}.
\]

The next sum must be a square larger than \(4\).  
If the next square is \((2k)^{2}\) (even), the gap would be a multiple
of \(4\), impossible because all divisors of \(m\) are odd.
Hence the next square must be odd, i.e. \((2k+1)^{2}\) with \(k\ge 1\).

The corresponding gap is  

\[
(2k+1)^{2}-4 = (2k-1)(2k+3)=4k+1\equiv 1\pmod 4 .
\tag{4}
\]

Thus the third divisor has to be \(\equiv1\pmod4\); the smallest such
odd divisor is \(5\).  Consequently \(5\mid m\).

Now the sum would be  

\[
4+5=9=3^{2}.
\]

Repeating the same argument forces the next divisor to be
\(\equiv3\pmod4\), the smallest possibility being \(7\); then
\(7\mid m\), and so on.

Hence, after using the divisor \(3\) we are forced to have the whole
chain  

\[
1,\,3,\,5,\,7,\,9,\,11,\dots
\]

as divisors of \(m\).  That is impossible, because a positive integer
cannot contain all those distinct odd numbers as divisors unless it is at
least their product, which would already exceed the number itself.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 6.  The only way out  

The contradiction shows that the chain cannot contain **more than two**
divisors.  Therefore \(m\) must have exactly two divisors, i.e. \(m\) is
prime.

For a prime \(p\) the divisors are \(\{1,p\}\); the required ordering is
possible iff  

\[
1+p \text{ is a perfect square}.
\]

Write \(1+p = t^{2}\;(t\ge 2)\).  Then \(p=t^{2}-1=(t-1)(t+1)\).
Since \(p\) is prime, one factor must be \(1\); hence \(t-1=1\) and
\(t=2\).  Consequently \(p=2^{2}-1=3\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 7.  Verification  

For \(m=3\) the divisors are \(1,3\) and  

\[
1 = 1^{2},\qquad 1+3 = 4 = 2^{2},
\]

so the required permutation exists.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
