        \[
\boxed{m=3}
\]

**Explanation**

Let  

* \(D(m)=\{d_{1},\dots ,d_{n}\}\) be the set of all positive divisors of \(m\)  
* \(\sigma(m)=\sum_{d\mid m}d\) – the sum of the divisors.  

If a permutation \((a_{1},\dots ,a_{n})\) of \(D(m)\) satisfies  

\[
S_i=a_{1}+a_{2}+\dots +a_{i}\quad\text{is a perfect square for every }i,
\]

then, writing \(S_i=t_i^{2}\) (\(t_i\in\mathbb Z_{\ge 0}\)), we have  

\[
t_{0}=0,\qquad t_{n}^{2}=S_{n}=\sigma(m) .
\tag{1}
\]

Hence \(\sigma(m)\) itself must be a perfect square; put \(\sigma(m)=u^{2}\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  The first term

Since \(1\) is always a divisor of \(m\), the smallest square that can be
obtained is \(1^{2}=1\); otherwise the first difference would be at least
\(2\), which is not a divisor of any integer \(m>1\).
Thus

\[
a_{1}=1,\qquad t_{1}=1 .
\tag{2}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Parity considerations

Write \(t_{i-1}=x,\;t_{i}=y\;(y>x)\).  
The \(i\)-th difference is  

\[
a_{i}=y^{2}-x^{2}=(y-x)(y+x) .
\tag{3}
\]

The two factors \((y-x)\) and \((y+x)\) have the same parity; consequently
every divisor that appears as a difference is a product of two integers of
the same parity.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Odd \(m\)

Assume that \(m\) is odd and \(m>3\).  
All its divisors are odd, so every difference \(a_{i}\) is odd.
From (3) an odd difference forces \(y\) to be **even** (because
\(y-x\) and \(y+x\) must both be odd).  
The second square therefore is \(t_{2}=2\) and  

\[
a_{2}=t_{2}^{2}-t_{1}^{2}=4-1=3 .
\]

Thus the divisor \(3\) must belong to \(D(m)\); consequently \(3\mid m\).

Now \(t_{2}=2\) is even.  The next difference would be  

\[
a_{3}=t_{3}^{2}-t_{2}^{2}=t_{3}^{2}-4 .
\]

Since all divisors are odd, \(a_{3}\) must be odd, but for any integer
\(t_{3}\) the product \((t_{3}-2)(t_{3}+2)\) is even; therefore an odd
difference cannot occur after the square \(2^{2}\).  This contradicts the
assumption that all divisors of an odd \(m>3\) can be used.

Hence the only odd \(m\) that can work is the case where the set of
divisors consists solely of \(\{1,3\}\); that is \(m=3\).  
Indeed

\[
1,\;3\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
0\;(=0^{2}),\;1\;(=1^{2}),\;4\;(=2^{2})
\]

gives the required permutation.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Even \(m\)

If \(m\) is even, the divisor \(2\) belongs to \(D(m)\).
From (3) a difference equal to \(2\) would require two consecutive squares
to differ by \(2\), which is impossible because

\[
(k+1)^{2}-k^{2}=2k+1\ge 3\quad\text{for all }k\ge 1 .
\]

Thus the divisor \(2\) can never appear as a difference, so no even
integer \(m\) satisfies the condition.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Verification for \(m=3\)

Divisors of \(3\): \(\{1,3\}\).  
The ordering \((1,3)\) yields the partial sums  

\[
1=1^{2},\qquad 1+3=4=2^{2},
\]

which are perfect squares.  Hence \(m=3\) indeed works.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 6.  Conclusion

The only positive integer \(m>1\) for which the divisors can be ordered
so that every initial sum is a perfect square is  

\[
\boxed{m=3}.
\]

