        [Turn 7 REASONING] (3807 chars)
        \[
\text{Find all }m>1\text{ for which the divisors of }m\text{ can be ordered }
(a_1,\dots ,a_n)
\]
so that  

\[
S_i=a_1+\cdots +a_i\qquad (1\le i\le n)
\]

is a perfect square.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Reformulation  

Put  

\[
S_i=t_i^{\,2}\qquad (t_0=0<t_1<t_2<\dots <t_n),
\]

so that  

\[
a_i=S_i-S_{i-1}=t_i^{\,2}-t_{i-1}^{\,2}
      =(t_i-t_{i-1})(t_i+t_{i-1}) . \tag{1}
\]

Thus every divisor of \(m\) must be representable as a product of two
integers of the same parity.
Conversely, if a divisor can be written as in (1) for some
\(t_{i-1}<t_i\), we may place it at the \(i\)-th position.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Immediate necessary conditions  

* \(a_1\) must be a perfect square, because \(t_0=0\).  
  Hence \(a_1=1\) and \(t_1=1\).

* The total sum of all divisors is \(\sigma(m)=t_n^{\,2}\); therefore  

  \[
  \sigma(m)\text{ is a perfect square.} \tag{2}
  \]

* If an even divisor \(d\) occurs, write \(d=(u-t)(u+t)\) with
  \(u>t\).  Both factors have the same parity, so an even product forces
  both to be even, i.e. \(d\) is a multiple of \(4\).
  Consequently a divisor congruent to \(2\pmod 4\) can never appear.
  In particular \(2\mid m\) is impossible, so **\(m\) must be odd**.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  How a given odd divisor can be used  

Let \(d\) be an odd divisor of \(m\).
Write \(d=xy\) with \(x\le y\) and \(x,y\) odd.
From (1) we obtain  

\[
t=\frac{y-x}{2},\qquad u=\frac{y+x}{2},
\]
so that the divisor \(d\) can be taken exactly when the current
square is \(t^{2}\).

If we choose the *minimal* factorisation \(x=1,\;y=d\) we get  

\[
t=\frac{d-1}{2},\qquad u=\frac{d+1}{2},
\]
i.e. \(d=(t+1)^{2}-t^{2}=2t+1\).  
Thus the divisor \(d\) can be used **only when the present square is
\(t^{2}\) with \(t=(d-1)/2\)**.  

If we use a non‑minimal factorisation, then \(x\ge 3\) and

\[
d=xy\ge 3(2t+3)=6t+9>2t+1 .
\]

Consequently such a divisor could be taken only *before* we ever reach
the square \(t^{2}\); after we have passed \(t\) it can never be used
again.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  The order of the divisors is forced  

We already have \(a_1=1\) and we are at \(t_1=1\).
The next divisor must be the one that can be used at \(t=1\), namely  

\[
2\cdot1+1=3 .
\]

Hence \(a_2=3\) and we reach \(t_2=2\) (\(S_2=4\)).
Repeating the argument, the only divisor that can be used at
\(t=2\) is \(2\cdot2+1=5\); therefore \(a_3=5\) and we move to
\(t_3=3\) (\(S_3=9\)).  

Inductively we obtain  

\[
a_i = 2t_{i-1}+1,\qquad t_i = t_{i-1}+1 .
\]

Thus the whole list of divisors must be  

\[
\{1,3,5,\dots ,2k-1\}
\]

for some \(k\ge 2\); the final square is \(t_k=k\) and
\(\sigma(m)=k^{2}\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Which \(m\) have exactly those divisors?  

If the set of divisors of an integer \(m\) is precisely the set of
all odd numbers from \(1\) up to \(2k-1\), then every odd number \(\le
2k-1\) must divide \(m\).  That forces  

\[
m\ge \operatorname{lcm}\bigl\{1,3,5,\dots ,2k-1\bigr\}>2k-1,
\]

a contradiction unless the set contains only the two numbers
\(1\) and \(3\).  Hence the only possible case is \(k=2\), i.e.

\[
\text{divisors}(m)=\{1,3\}\Longrightarrow m=3 .
\]

(The case \(k=1\) would give \(m=1\), which is excluded by the
statement.)

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 6.  Verification  

For \(m=3\) the divisors are \(1,3\) and  

\[
1,\;1+3=4=2^{2}
\]

satisfy the required condition.  

All other \(m>1\) fail, as shown above.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
\[
\boxed{\,m=3\,}
\]

