        [Turn 11 REASONING] (4430 chars)
        \[
\text{Find all }m>1\text{ for which the divisors of }m\text{ can be ordered }
(a_{1},a_{2},\dots ,a_{n})
\]
\[
\text{so that }S_i=a_{1}+a_{2}+\dots +a_{i}\text{ is a perfect square for every }i .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 1.  A necessary parity condition  

If \(x\) is a difference of two squares, \(x=y^{2}-z^{2}=(y-z)(y+z)\).
Both factors have the same parity, therefore  

\[
x\equiv 0,1,3\pmod 4\qquad\text{and }x\not\equiv 2\pmod 4 .
\]

Consequently **no divisor of \(m\) may be \(\equiv2\pmod4\)**.  
If \(m\) were even, the divisor \(2\) would be present, a contradiction.
Hence  

\[
\boxed{m\text{ is odd}} .
\]

All divisors of an odd integer are odd, so every required difference
\(a_i\) is odd.  
For an odd difference we must have the two consecutive squares of opposite
parity; therefore the sequence of squares must alternate parity:

\[
S_{1}\;(odd),\;S_{2}\;(multiple\;of\;4),\;S_{3}\;(odd),\dots
\tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 2.  The smallest prime divisor

Let \(p\) be the smallest prime divisor of \(m\) (\(p\ge 3\)).
In the required ordering the number \(p\) occurs somewhere.
Because the squares alternate parity, the two squares that surround \(p\)
have opposite parity, hence \(p\) is the **difference of two consecutive
squares**:

\[
p = y^{2}-x^{2}=(y-x)(y+x).
\]

Since \(p\) is prime, the only factorisation into odd factors is
\(1\cdot p\); thus  

\[
y-x=1,\qquad y+x=p\Longrightarrow
y=\frac{p+1}{2},\;x=\frac{p-1}{2}.
\tag{2}
\]

Consequently the two consecutive partial sums must be  

\[
x^{2}= \Bigl(\frac{p-1}{2}\Bigr)^{2},\qquad
y^{2}= \Bigl(\frac{p+1}{2}\Bigr)^{2}.
\]

Before the divisor \(p\) is added, only divisors **smaller than \(p\)**
may have been used.  
But \(p\) is the smallest prime divisor, so the only possible divisor
smaller than \(p\) is \(1\).  
Hence the sum just before \(p\) can only be \(1\); therefore  

\[
1 =\Bigl(\frac{p-1}{2}\Bigr)^{2}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad p=3 .
\]

Thus the smallest prime divisor of a suitable \(m\) is forced to be  

\[
\boxed{p=3}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 3.  What happens after the first two terms  

Take the ordering that begins with the square divisor \(1\) and then the
prime divisor \(3\).  
The first two partial sums are  

\[
S_{1}=1=1^{2},\qquad S_{2}=1+3=4=2^{2}.
\]

Because of the alternating parity (1), the next partial sum must be an
odd square, the smallest one being \(3^{2}=9\).  
Hence the third divisor must be  

\[
a_{3}=9-4=5 .
\tag{3}
\]

So **\(5\) must divide \(m\)**.

Now \(S_{3}=9\). The next square must be even; the smallest such square
greater than \(9\) is \(4^{2}=16\). Consequently  

\[
a_{4}=16-9=7,
\]

so **\(7\) must also divide \(m\)**.

Continuing in this way we obtain the inevitable chain  

\[
\begin{aligned}
S_{4}&=16, & a_{5}=25-16&=9,\\
S_{5}&=25, & a_{6}=36-25&=11,\\
S_{6}&=36, & a_{7}=49-36&=13,\ \dots
\end{aligned}
\]

In general, after the even square \((2k)^{2}\) the next odd square is
\((2k+1)^{2}\); the required difference is  

\[
(2k+1)^{2}-(2k)^{2}=4k+1,
\]

the **consecutive odd numbers** \(3,5,7,9,11,\dots\).

Thus, **every odd integer \(2k+1\) that appears before the final sum
must be a divisor of \(m\)**.  
If \(m\) possessed any divisor larger than \(3\), the process would force
the presence of the next odd integer, then the next one, and so on,
requiring \(m\) to be divisible by **all** odd numbers up to a certain
size – an impossibility for a finite integer.

The only way to avoid this infinite chain is that the process stops
immediately after the second term, i.e. the total sum of all divisors is
already \(S_{2}=4\).  Hence  

\[
\sigma(m)=1+3=4 .
\]

The only odd integer whose divisor‑sum equals \(4\) is \(m=3\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 4.  Verification

Divisors of \(m=3\) are \(\{1,3\}\).  
The ordering \((1,3)\) gives  

\[
S_{1}=1=1^{2},\qquad S_{2}=1+3=4=2^{2},
\]

so the condition is satisfied.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 5.  Conclusion  

The reasoning above shows that any integer \(m>1\) satisfying the
required property must be odd, must have smallest prime divisor \(3\), and
must not contain any divisor larger than \(3\); consequently \(m=3\).

\[
\boxed{\,m=3\,}
\]

