        [Turn 10 REASONING] (4568 chars)
        \[
\text{Find all }m>1\text{ for which the divisors of }m\text{ can be ordered }
(a_{1},a_{2},\dots ,a_{n})
\]
\[
\text{so that }S_{i}=a_{1}+a_{2}+\dots +a_{i}\;(1\le i\le n)
\text{ is a perfect square.}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Preliminary observations  

* \(1\) is always a divisor, therefore \(S_{1}=a_{1}\) must be a square.
  Hence we may (and shall) take \(a_{1}=1\) and \(S_{1}=1\).

* Let \(S_{i}=x_{i}^{2}\;(x_{i}>0)\).  
  Then  

  \[
  a_{i}=S_{i}-S_{i-1}=x_{i}^{2}-x_{i-1}^{2}
          =(x_{i}-x_{i-1})(x_{i}+x_{i-1}) .\tag{1}
  \]

  Consequently every divisor of \(m\) is a **difference of two squares**.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  \(m\) cannot be even  

If \(m\) is even, the divisor \(2\) occurs.  
From (1) we would need  

\[
2=x^{2}-y^{2}=(x-y)(x+y) .
\]

The only factorisation of \(2\) into two positive integers is \(1\cdot 2\);
solving \(x-y=1,\;x+y=2\) gives \(x=\tfrac32\not\in\mathbb Z\).  
Thus a difference of two squares can never be \(2\).  
Hence **\(m\) must be odd**.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  The smallest divisor after \(1\)  

Let \(p\) be the smallest prime divisor of \(m\) (\(p\ge 3\)).  
Because \(p\) is a divisor, there are squares \(x^{2}>y^{2}\) with  

\[
p=x^{2}-y^{2}=(x-y)(x+y). \tag{2}
\]

Since \(p\) is prime, one of the factors in (2) must be \(1\) and the
other \(p\).  Hence  

\[
x-y=1,\qquad x+y=p\Longrightarrow
x=\frac{p+1}{2},\;y=\frac{p-1}{2}. \tag{3}
\]

Consequently the **first two partial sums are**

\[
S_{1}=1,\qquad S_{2}=1+p=x^{2}= \Bigl(\frac{p+1}{2}\Bigr)^{2}. \tag{4}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  The number of divisors  

Because \(m\) is odd, all its divisors are odd.
Thus each difference \(a_{i}=S_{i}-S_{i-1}\) is odd, and from (1) the
parities of successive squares alternate:
odd – even – odd – … .
Since the first square \(S_{1}=1\) is odd, the last square
\(S_{n}= \sigma(m)\) (the sum of all divisors) is also odd.
Therefore the total number of divisors of an odd integer,
\(\tau(m)\), is **odd**; consequently the number of *proper*
divisors (excluding \(1\) and \(m\)) is even.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  \(m\) must be prime  

Assume that \(m\) is composite.  
Write \(m=p\cdot q\) with \(q>1\) ( \(q\) is also odd).
The divisor \(q\) must appear somewhere in the sequence.
Let the partial sum just before adding \(q\) be \(u^{2}\); after adding
\(q\) we obtain \(v^{2}=u^{2}+q\).  From (1)

\[
q=v^{2}-u^{2}=(v-u)(v+u). \tag{5}
\]

Both factors on the right‑hand side are odd, hence they are
\(\ge 3\).  Consequently \(q\ge 3\cdot 3=9\); in particular
\(q\neq p\) and \(q\) is **not** the smallest divisor after \(1\).

Now look at the two smallest proper divisors of \(m\): they are
\(p\) and the next one, say \(d\;(d>p)\).
From (4) we already have \(S_{2}=1+p\); the next square must be

\[
S_{3}=S_{2}+d = (x^{2}+d)=y^{2}.
\]

Thus \(d=y^{2}-x^{2}=(y-x)(y+x)\) with both factors odd.
Since \(d>p\) and \(p\) is the smallest odd divisor,
the only way for an odd integer to be expressed as a product of two
odd numbers larger than \(1\) is to be **composite**.
Hence \(d\) is composite, and therefore \(m\) possesses at least three
distinct proper divisors:
\(p,\;d,\;q\).

But the number of proper divisors is even (see §4), a contradiction.
Hence the assumption that \(m\) is composite is impossible; **\(m\) must be
prime**.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 6.  Prime case  

Let \(m\) be an odd prime.  
Its set of divisors is \(\{1,m\}\); the sum of all divisors is
\(\sigma(m)=1+m\).  
For the required ordering we need

\[
1\;(=1^{2}),\qquad 1+m\;(=k^{2})\quad\text{for some }k\in\mathbb N .
\]

Thus \(m=k^{2}-1=(k-1)(k+1)\).  
Since \(m\) is prime, one of the factors must be \(1\); consequently
\(k-1=1\) and \(k=2\).  Hence \(m=2^{2}-1=3\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 7.  Verification  

Divisors of \(3\): \(\{1,3\}\).

\[
a_{1}=1,\;S_{1}=1=1^{2},\qquad
a_{2}=3,\;S_{2}=1+3=4=2^{2}.
\]

All partial sums are perfect squares, so \(m=3\) indeed satisfies the
condition.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 8.  Conclusion  

The only integer \(m>1\) for which the divisors of \(m\) can be
permuted so that every initial partial sum is a perfect square is  

\[
\boxed{\,m=3\,}.
\]

