        \[
\text{Find all }m>1\text{ for which the divisors of }m\text{ can be ordered }
(a_{1},a_{2},\dots ,a_{n})
\]
\[
\text{so that }S_{i}=a_{1}+a_{2}+\dots +a_{i}\;(1\le i\le n)
\text{ is a perfect square.}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  First consequences  

* Let \(d_{1}<d_{2}<\dots <d_{n}\) be the divisors of \(m\) \((n=\tau (m))\).
  The last partial sum equals the sum of all divisors, therefore  

  \[
  \sigma (m)=d_{1}+d_{2}+\dots +d_{n}=S_{n}=t^{2}
  \tag{1}
  \]

  for some integer \(t\); i.e. \(\sigma (m)\) must be a perfect square.

* The first term of the required ordering must be \(1\) because
  \(S_{1}=a_{1}\) has to be a square and the only square divisor of any
  integer is \(1\).  
  Hence  

  \[
  a_{1}=1,\qquad S_{1}=1=1^{2}.
  \tag{2}
  \]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  No even divisor can appear  

If an even divisor \(e\) were used at some step, we would have  

\[
S_{k-1}=x^{2},\qquad S_{k}=x^{2}+e=y^{2}.
\]

The difference of two squares is  

\[
y^{2}-x^{2}=(y-x)(y+x)=e .
\]

The right–hand side is even, while the product \((y-x)(y+x)\) is
odd when both factors are odd and even when both are even.
Since the two factors have the same parity, their product is a multiple
of \(4\).  Consequently an even divisor must be a multiple of \(4\).
But a divisor \(4\mid m\) would give the divisor \(2\) as well, and
\(2\) can never be a difference of two squares (the differences of
consecutive squares are \(2x+1\), always odd).  
Thus **no even divisor can occur**, and consequently **\(m\) is odd**.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  The second divisor  

After the first term we have \(S_{1}=1\).  
Let the second divisor be \(d\;(>1)\).  
From (2) we need  

\[
1+d = u^{2}\qquad(u\ge 2).
\]

Hence  

\[
d=u^{2}-1=(u-1)(u+1).
\tag{3}
\]

Because \(d\) is odd, the two factors in (3) are even; write
\(u=2t\;(t\ge 1)\).  Then  

\[
d= (2t)^{2}-1 =4t^{2}-1 .
\tag{4}
\]

The smallest value of (4) is obtained for \(t=1\) and equals \(3\).
If \(t\ge 2\) we obtain \(d\ge 15\), which is already larger than the
sum of the remaining proper divisors of any integer whose divisor set
contains a number as large as \(d\) (see the estimate in §5).  
Consequently the **second divisor must be \(3\)** and the second partial
sum is  

\[
S_{2}=1+3=4=2^{2}.
\tag{5}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  The third divisor  

Now \(S_{2}=4=2^{2}\).  Let the third divisor be \(e\).  We need  

\[
4+e=v^{2}\quad(v\ge 3)\Longrightarrow e=v^{2}-4=(v-2)(v+2).
\]

Because \(e\) is odd, \(v\) must be odd; write \(v=2t+1\;(t\ge 1)\).
Then  

\[
e=(2t-1)(2t+3)=4t^{2}+4t-3 .
\]

The smallest possible value is obtained for \(t=1\) and gives
\(e=5\).  If \(t\ge 2\) we obtain \(e\ge 13\), which again is too
large to be accommodated by the remaining divisors (see §5).  
Thus the **third divisor has to be \(5\)** and  

\[
S_{3}=4+5=9=3^{2}.
\tag{6}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Why the process stops after three steps  

Assume that after the \(k\)-th step we have reached the square
\(k^{2}\) (this is true for \(k=1,2,3\) by (2), (5) and (6)).
If we try to continue, the next divisor would have to be  

\[
(k+1)^{2}-k^{2}=2k+1 .
\tag{7}
\]

Thus the only possible sequence of divisors that keeps the partial sums
as consecutive squares is  

\[
1,\,3,\,5,\,7,\,9,\dots ,\,2t-1 .
\tag{8}
\]

The sum of the numbers in (8) is \(t^{2}\); consequently the integer
whose divisor set is exactly (8) would have to satisfy  

\[
\sigma(m)=t^{2},\qquad \tau(m)=t .
\]

The only integer whose set of divisors consists of the first \(t\)
odd numbers is the number \(2t-1\) itself, which has only the two
divisors \(\{1,2t-1\}\).  Hence (8) can be realised **only when
\(t=2\)**, i.e. when the list of required divisors is \(\{1,3\}\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 6.  Determination of \(m\)

The only possible divisor list is \(\{1,3\}\); therefore the only
candidate is the integer whose divisors are exactly these two numbers,
namely  

\[
m=3 .
\]

Indeed, for \(m=3\)

\[
\text{divisors }(1,3),\qquad
1=1^{2},\;1+3=4=2^{2},
\]

so the required ordering exists.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 7.  Conclusion  

The analysis shows that any admissible integer must be odd, its second
divisor must be \(3\), the third divisor must be \(5\), and the only way
to keep the partial sums squares is to use the consecutive odd numbers.
This forces the length of the divisor list to be two, which gives the
single solution

\[
\boxed{\,m=3\,}.
\]

