*If \(m\) is even, the divisor \(2\) occurs.  
A number that is \(2\pmod 4\) cannot be written as a difference of two squares,  
hence it can never be added to a square to obtain another square.  
Thus an admissible \(m\) must be **odd**.*

Consequently every divisor of \(m\) is odd.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  The first two terms  

The first partial sum must be the smallest square, i.e. \(S_{1}=1\); therefore  

\[
a_{1}=1 .
\]

Let the second partial sum be \(S_{2}=k^{2}\;(k\ge 2)\).  
Then  

\[
a_{2}=S_{2}-S_{1}=k^{2}-1=(k-1)(k+1).
\]

Because \(a_{2}\) is a divisor of the odd integer \(m\), it is odd; the only
odd divisor of the form \(k^{2}-1\) that can be prime is obtained for \(k=2\):
\(a_{2}=3\).  
Hence the **smallest prime divisor of \(m\) is \(3\)** and we must have  

\[
a_{2}=3,\qquad S_{2}=4 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  The residues of the remaining divisors  

From now on the partial sums are squares of integers whose parity alternates
(because the difference of two squares is odd).  
Since \(S_{2}=2^{2}\) is even, the next square must be odd, say \((2t+1)^{2}\)
with \(t\ge 1\).  Its difference from \(S_{2}\) is  

\[
(2t+1)^{2}-4 =4t(t+1)-3\equiv 5\pmod 8 .
\]

Thus **every divisor that is added after the first two must be \(\equiv5\pmod8\).**
Consequently all divisors of \(m\) other than \(1\) and \(3\) are \(\equiv5\pmod8\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  The shape of \(m\)

Let \(p\) be a prime divisor of \(m\) different from \(3\).  
From the previous paragraph \(p\equiv5\pmod8\).

* If \(p^{2}\mid m\) then \(p^{2}\equiv1\pmod8\) would be a divisor of \(m\),
  contradicting the requirement that every divisor \(\neq1,3\) be
  \(\equiv5\pmod8\).  Hence the exponent of any such prime is \(1\).

* If \(m\) possessed two distinct primes \(p,q\equiv5\pmod8\), then
  \(pq\) would be a divisor and \(pq\equiv1\pmod8\), again impossible.

Therefore the only possibilities are  

\[
m=3\qquad\text{or}\qquad m=3p\;(p\equiv5\pmod8\text{ prime}).
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Eliminate the case \(m=3p\)

For \(m=3p\) we have  

\[
\sigma(m)=\sigma(3)\sigma(p)=(1+3)(1+p)=4(p+1).
\]

The total sum of the divisors must be a square, say \(\sigma(m)=S^{2}\).
Since \(\sigma(m)\) is a multiple of \(4\), write \(S=2t\); then  

\[
4(p+1)=4t^{2}\Longrightarrow p+1=t^{2}\Longrightarrow p=t^{2}-1=(t-1)(t+1).
\]

Because \(p\) is prime, we must have \(t-1=1\), i.e. \(t=2\) and \(p=3\),
which contradicts \(p\equiv5\pmod8\).  Hence **no number of the form \(3p\) works**.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 6.  The only admissible integer  

The only remaining candidate is \(m=3\).  
Its divisors are \(\{1,3\}\) and the ordering  

\[
(1,3)
\]

gives the partial sums \(1,\,1+3=4\), both perfect squares.  

--------------------------------------------------------------------
\[
\boxed{\,m=3\,}
\]

Thus the positive integer \(m>1\) for which the divisors can be permuted so that
every initial sum is a perfect square is uniquely \(m=3\).

