        \[
\text{Find all }m>1\text{ for which the divisors of }m\text{ can be ordered }
(a_{1},a_{2},\dots ,a_{n})
\]
\[
\text{so that }S_{i}=a_{1}+a_{2}+\dots +a_{i}\;(1\le i\le n)
\text{ is a perfect square.}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  What the condition implies for the whole sum  

Let  

\[
d(m)=\tau (m)=\#\{\text{divisors of }m\},\qquad 
\sigma (m)=\sum_{d\mid m}d .
\]

If the required ordering exists, then the last partial sum equals the
sum of **all** divisors, hence  

\[
S_{n}= \sigma (m) \text{ is a perfect square.}
\]

Write \(\sigma (m)=T^{2}\) with \(T\in \mathbb {N}\).
The partial sums are the squares  

\[
1^{2}=S_{1}<S_{2}<\dots <S_{n}=T^{2},
\]

so there are exactly \(n\) distinct squares between \(1^{2}\) and
\(T^{2}\).  Consequently  

\[
n=T .
\]

Thus  

\[
\tau (m)=T=\sqrt{\sigma (m)}\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad 
\sigma (m)=\tau (m)^{2}. \tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Reducing (1) to a multiplicative equation  

Define  

\[
f(m)=\frac{\sigma (m)}{\tau (m)^{2}} .
\]

Both \(\sigma\) and \(\tau\) are multiplicative, therefore \(f\) is also
multiplicative:

\[
f(ab)=f(a)f(b)\quad\text{whenever }(a,b)=1 .
\]

For a prime power \(p^{k}\;(k\ge 1)\)

\[
\begin{aligned}
\sigma(p^{k})&=1+p+\dots +p^{k}= \frac{p^{k+1}-1}{p-1},\\[2mm]
\tau(p^{k})&=k+1,
\end{aligned}
\]

hence  

\[
f(p^{k})=\frac{p^{k+1}-1}{(p-1)(k+1)^{2}}. \tag{2}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Values of \(f(p^{k})\)

*For \(p=3,\;k=1\)*  

\[
f(3)=\frac{3^{2}-1}{(3-1)2^{2}}=\frac{8}{8}=1 .
\]

*For \(p=2\)*  

\[
f(2^{k})=\frac{2^{k+1}-1}{(k+1)^{2}}
          =\begin{cases}
          \dfrac34 & (k=1),\\[2mm]
          \dfrac79 & (k=2),\\[2mm]
          \dfrac{15}{16}& (k=3),\\[2mm]
          >1 & (k\ge 4).
          \end{cases}
\]

Thus \(f(2^{k})<1\) only for \(k=1,2,3\).

*For any other prime \(p\ge 5\) and any \(k\ge 1\)*  

\[
p^{k+1}-1\ge p^{2}-1\ge 24,\qquad (p-1)(k+1)^{2}\le (p-1)4,
\]

which gives \(f(p^{k})>1\).  
For \(p=3\) and \(k\ge 2\) we also have \(f(3^{k})>1\).

Consequently

\[
\boxed{\;f(p^{k})=1\iff (p,k)=(3,1)\;},
\]
and the only factors of \(f\) that are **smaller** than \(1\) are
\(f(2),f(2^{2}),f(2^{3})\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Solving \(f(m)=1\)

Because \(f\) is multiplicative,
\(f(m)=1\) means the product of the values \(f(p^{k})\) over the
different prime powers of \(m\) equals \(1\).

*If a factor \(f(p^{k})>1\) occurs, it must be cancelled by a factor
\(<1\).  The only possible factors \(<1\) are those coming from
\(2^{k}\;(k=1,2,3)\).  Direct computation shows*

\[
\begin{aligned}
f(2)   &=\frac34,\\
f(2^{2})&=\frac79,\\
f(2^{3})&=\frac{15}{16},
\end{aligned}
\qquad
f(p^{k})\ge \frac{5}{4}\;(p\ge 5\text{ or }p=3,k\ge 2).
\]

Multiplying any of the numbers \(\frac34,\frac79,\frac{15}{16}\) by a
number \(\ge\frac54\) never yields \(1\); the product is always
\(<1\) or \(>1\).  Hence a factor \(>1\) cannot be balanced, and therefore
**no factor \(>1\) may appear**.

Thus every prime‑power factor of \(m\) must satisfy \(f(p^{k})=1\), which,
as shown above, forces \((p,k)=(3,1)\).

Hence  

\[
m=3 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Verification that \(m=3\) works  

Divisors of \(3\): \(\{1,3\}\).

Take the order \((a_{1},a_{2})=(1,3)\).

\[
a_{1}=1=1^{2},\qquad a_{1}+a_{2}=1+3=4=2^{2}.
\]

Both partial sums are perfect squares, so the required permutation
exists.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 6.  Answer  

The only integer \(m>1\) for which the divisors of \(m\) can be
permuted so that every initial sum is a perfect square is  

\[
\boxed{\,m=3\,}.
\]

