        \[
\text{Find all }m>1\text{ for which the divisors of }m\text{ can be ordered }
(a_{1},a_{2},\dots ,a_{n})
\]
\[
\text{so that }S_{i}=a_{1}+a_{2}+\dots +a_{i}\;(1\le i\le n)
\text{ is a perfect square.}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.   First necessary facts  

*Let \(D(m)\) be the set of all positive divisors of \(m\) and
\(n=|D(m)|\).  Put  

\[
\sigma(m)=\sum_{d\mid m}d .
\]

Because \(S_{n}\) is the sum of all divisors,  

\[
S_{n}= \sigma(m)=t^{2}\qquad (t\in \mathbb Z_{>0}). \tag{1}
\]

Hence \(\sigma(m)\) must be a perfect square.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.   No divisor can be congruent to \(2\pmod 4\)

If a number \(x\) can be written as a difference of two squares,
\(x=b^{2}-a^{2}=(b-a)(b+a)\), then the two factors \(b-a\) and
\(b+a\) have the same parity; consequently a number of the form
\(2\pmod 4\) **cannot** be a difference of two squares.
Every term \(a_{i}\) is a difference of two consecutive squares
\(S_{i-1}\) and \(S_{i}\); therefore no divisor of \(m\) may be
\(2\pmod 4\).  The only way this can happen is that **\(m\) is odd**.
Consequently all divisors of \(m\) are odd.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.   The first and the last terms are forced  

*The first term.*  
\(S_{0}=0\) is a square.  If the first term were a divisor \(d>1\),
then \(d=S_{1}\) would have to be a square.  The only square divisor
of an odd integer is \(1\).  Hence  

\[
a_{1}=1,\qquad S_{1}=1. \tag{2}
\]

*The last term.*  
Let \(a_{n}=m\) (the largest divisor).  If \(m\) were placed before the
end, after adding any further positive divisor the total would exceed
\(\sigma(m)\), contradicting (1).  Thus \(m\) must be the last term,
and  

\[
S_{n-1}= \sigma(m)-m \text{ is a square}. \tag{3}
\]

Write  

\[
\sigma(m)=t^{2},\qquad \sigma(m)-m=s^{2}\;(s<t).
\]

From (1)–(3)

\[
m=t^{2}-s^{2}=(t-s)(t+s). \tag{4}
\]

Because \(m\) is odd, both factors \(t-s\) and \(t+s\) are odd.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.   Reduction to the case of a prime divisor  

Let \(p\) be the smallest prime divisor of \(m\) (\(p\ge 3\)).
From (2) we have \(S_{1}=1\) and the next square is some even square
\(k^{2}>1\).  The second term is  

\[
a_{2}=k^{2}-1=(k-1)(k+1).
\]

Since \(k\) must be even (otherwise the product would be a multiple of
\(8\) and could not divide the odd number \(m\)), we have
\(k=2r\) and  

\[
a_{2}=4r^{2}-1\equiv 3\pmod 4 .
\]

Thus the second term is a divisor of \(m\) congruent to \(3\pmod 4\);
consequently the smallest prime divisor of \(m\) is \(p=3\).  Hence

\[
3\mid m . \tag{5}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.   Eliminating composite multiples of \(3\)

Write \(m=3q\) with \(q\) odd.  
Because of (4) we have a factorisation \(m=(t-s)(t+s)\) with two odd
factors.  One of them is divisible by \(3\); let  

\[
t-s=3u,\qquad t+s=v\;(u,v\text{ odd}).
\]

Then  

\[
t=\frac{v+3u}{2},\qquad s=\frac{v-3u}{2},
\qquad \sigma(m)=t^{2}= \frac{(v+3u)^{2}}{4}.
\]

Using \(\sigma(m)=\sum_{d\mid m}d\) and the well‑known formula  

\[
\sigma(3q)=\sigma(3)\sigma(q)=4\,\sigma(q),
\]

equation \(\sigma(m)=t^{2}\) becomes  

\[
4\,\sigma(q)=\frac{(v+3u)^{2}}{4}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
16\,\sigma(q)=(v+3u)^{2}. \tag{6}
\]

The left‑hand side of (6) is a multiple of \(16\); therefore
\(v+3u\) is a multiple of \(4\).  Since \(v\) and \(u\) are odd,
\(v+3u\equiv 0\pmod 4\) forces \(v\equiv u\equiv 1\pmod 4\).

Now \(\sigma(q)\) is odd (all divisors of the odd number \(q\) are
odd), so the right‑hand side of (6) is a square of an even integer.
Consequently \(\sigma(q)\) itself must be a perfect square.
Repeating the whole argument with the odd integer \(q\) we obtain
that the smallest prime divisor of \(q\) is also \(3\); hence \(9\mid m\).

Iterating, we would obtain that every power of \(3\) divides \(m\).
If \(3^{k}\mid m\) with \(k\ge 2\), then \(9\mid m\) and the divisor
\(9\) would have to appear somewhere in the sequence.
But after the first term \(1\) the only possible second term is a
divisor congruent to \(3\pmod 4\); the number \(9\equiv 1\pmod 4\) can
never occur as a second term, and it cannot occur later because the
partial sum just before it would be a square, and adding \(9\) to a
square never yields another square (the difference of two squares is
never \(9\)).  Hence \(9\) cannot be a divisor of a suitable \(m\).

Therefore the only possibility is that the power of \(3\) occurring in
\(m\) is exactly \(3^{1}\); i.e.  

\[
m=3. \tag{7}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 6.   Verification  

The divisors of \(3\) are \(\{1,3\}\).
The ordering \((1,3)\) gives the partial sums  

\[
1=1^{2},\qquad 1+3=4=2^{2},
\]

both perfect squares.  Hence \(m=3\) indeed satisfies the required
property.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 7.   Conclusion  

The discussion above shows that any integer \(m>1\) satisfying the
condition must be odd, must have \(\sigma(m)\) and \(\sigma(m)-m\) both
perfect squares, forces the smallest prime divisor to be \(3\), and
cannot contain the divisor \(9\).  The only integer meeting all these
requirements is \(m=3\).

\[
\boxed{\,m=3\,}
\]

