--- [combinatorics.extremal.grid_packing] ---
Triggers: place coins on grid with adjacency difference exactly 1, stacking coins on board, coin placement height constraint, adjacent squares differ by 1 coin, maximum board size for fixed coins
Technique: Grid packing/covering: To find minimum n objects covering an m×m grid with constraints: (1) Lower bound via pigeonhole — if each object covers at most k cells, need >= m²/k objects. (2) Upper bound via explicit construction — tile or pack optimally. For non-attacking rook-type placements: n-1 fails by pigeonhole on rows/columns, n succeeds by diagonal placement. For domino tiling: parity/coloring argument for impossibility, explicit construction for possibility.
Question: Place 471 coins in the squares of an \(n\times n\) board where each block can hold 1 coin, multiple coins, or no coin at all, such that the difference in the number of coins between any two adjacent squares (squares that share a common side) is 1. Find the maximum value of \(n\) for which such an arrangement is possible.
Answer: Place 471 coins on nxn board with adjacency constraints. Use pigeonhole to establish lower bound on n, then construct a valid configuration at that n. The construction involves distributing coins in a pattern where no two adjacent cells differ by more than 1.

