        [Turn 3 REASONING] (2850 chars)
        \[
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Let }a_{i,j}\ge 0\;(1\le i,j\le n) \text{ be the number of coins in the }(i,j)\text{-square.}\\
&|a_{i+1,j}-a_{i,j}|=|a_{i,j+1}-a_{i,j}|=1\qquad(\text{adjacent squares}).
\end{aligned}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Structure of all solutions  

Write  

\[
a_{i,j}=A_i+B_j\qquad (1\le i,j\le n)
\]

where  

\[
A_{i+1}-A_i=\pm1,\qquad B_{j+1}-B_j=\pm1 .
\]

Indeed, if we set \(A_1=0\) and define \(A_{i+1}=A_i+s_{i,i+1}\) with
\(s_{i,i+1}=a_{i+1,j}-a_{i,j}\in\{+1,-1\}\), the vertical condition forces the
same difference for every \(j\); similarly for the horizontal direction.
Conversely, any two sequences \((A_i)\) and \((B_j)\) with steps \(\pm1\) give a
grid satisfying the adjacency condition, because

\[
a_{i+1,j}-a_{i,j}=A_{i+1}-A_i=\pm1,\qquad
a_{i,j+1}-a_{i,j}=B_{j+1}-B_j=\pm1 .
\]

Thus **every admissible placement is of the form \(a_{i,j}=A_i+B_j\) with the
above step‑conditions**.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  The total number of coins  

Let  

\[
S=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}a_{i,j}
      =\sum_{i,j}(A_i+B_j)
      =n\Bigl(\sum_{i=1}^{n}A_i+\sum_{j=1}^{n}B_j\Bigr)
      =n\,(S_A+S_B),
\]

where \(S_A=\sum_i A_i\) and \(S_B=\sum_j B_j\) are integers.
Hence  

\[
\boxed{S\text{ must be a multiple of }n } .
\]

We are given \(S=471\).  
The divisors of \(471=3\cdot157\) not exceeding \(30\) (the obvious upper bound
\(\sqrt{2\cdot471}\approx 30\)) are

\[
n=1,\;3 .
\]

Therefore the only possible board sizes are \(n=1\) or \(n=3\); the maximum
candidate is \(n=3\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Existence for \(n=3\)

We must exhibit sequences \((A_i)_{i=1}^3,\,(B_j)_{j=1}^3\) with steps \(\pm1\)
such that  

\[
3\,(S_A+S_B)=471\;\Longrightarrow\;S_A+S_B=157,
\]
and \(A_i+B_j\ge0\) for all \(i,j\).

Take  

\[
A_1=51,\;A_2=52,\;A_3=53\qquad(\text{steps }+1,+1),
\]
\[
B_1=0,\;B_2=1,\;B_3=0\qquad(\text{steps }+1,-1).
\]

Then  

\[
S_A=51+52+53=156,\qquad S_B=0+1+0=1,
\]
so \(S_A+S_B=157\) and the total number of coins is  

\[
3\cdot157=471 .
\]

All entries \(A_i+B_j\) are non‑negative, and adjacent differences are
exactly \(1\). The resulting board is

\[
\begin{array}{ccc}
51 & 52 & 51\\
52 & 53 & 52\\
53 & 54 & 53
\end{array}
\]

which satisfies every requirement.

Thus a placement exists for \(n=3\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Maximality  

For any \(n>3\) we would need \(n\mid 471\), but the only divisors of
\(471\) not exceeding \(30\) are \(1\) and \(3\). Hence no board larger than
\(3\times3\) can accommodate exactly \(471\) coins under the given condition.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
\[
\boxed{\,\displaystyle n_{\max}=3\,}
\]

