        Let the squares of an \(n\times n\) board be coloured as a chessboard.
Denote the black squares by \(B\) and the white squares by \(W\).
If two adjacent squares differ by exactly one coin, then every black square
has the same parity (all even or all odd) and every white square has the
opposite parity.  
Consequently the numbers of coins on the two colour classes are of the
form  

\[
\begin{cases}
\text{black}=a,\qquad \text{white}=a+1 ,\\[2mm]
\text{or}\\[2mm]
\text{black}=a+1,\qquad \text{white}=a ,
\end{cases}
\qquad a\in\mathbb Z_{\ge 0}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Minimal possible total

Take \(a=0\).  
Then one colour class contains \(0\) coins in each of its squares and the
other colour class contains \(1\) coin in each of its squares.
Hence the smallest possible total number of coins is the number of squares
of the colour that receives the \(1\)’s:

\[
\boxed{\;L(n)=\Big\lfloor\frac{n^{2}}{2}\Big\rfloor\;}
\]

(For odd \(n\) this is \((n^{2}-1)/2\); for even \(n\) it is \(n^{2}/2\).)

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  How much can we increase the total?

In the minimal configuration the squares that contain \(0\) coins are all of
the same colour, and no two of them are adjacent.  
If we change any such square from \(0\) to \(2\) coins, each of its
neighbouring squares (which contain \(1\) coin) still differs by exactly
\(1\).  
Therefore we may raise **any subset** of the zero‑squares by \(2\) coins
without violating the adjacency condition.

If there are \(z\) zero‑squares, we can increase the total by any even
number between \(0\) and \(2z\).  For odd \(n\),

\[
z=\Big\lfloor\frac{n^{2}}{2}\Big\rfloor,\qquad
\text{so the attainable totals are}
\qquad L(n),\,L(n)+2,\,L(n)+4,\dots ,L(n)+2z .
\]

The other minimal pattern (swap the colours) gives the same interval but
shifted by one, so **every integer between**

\[
L(n)\quad\text{and}\quad L(n)+2z+1
\]

is attainable.  Since \(2z+1=n^{2}+1\),

\[
\boxed{\;L(n)\le S\le L(n)+n^{2}+1\;}
\]

and every integer in this interval can be realised.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Applying the bound to 471 coins

We need \(L(n)\le 471\).

*If \(n\) is even*: \(L(n)=\frac{n^{2}}{2}\) is even, while \(471\) is odd,
so an even‑sized board cannot work.

*If \(n\) is odd*: \(L(n)=\dfrac{n^{2}-1}{2}\le 471\)
\[
\Longrightarrow n^{2}\le 943\Longrightarrow n\le 30 .
\]

The next odd integer after \(30\) is \(31\); for \(n=31\),

\[
L(31)=\frac{31^{2}-1}{2}=480>471,
\]

so \(n=31\) is impossible.  Hence the largest possible odd size is
\(n=29\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  A concrete arrangement for \(n=29\)

Start with the checkerboard pattern

\[
\begin{array}{c}
\text{black squares : }1\text{ coin}\\
\text{white squares : }0\text{ coin}
\end{array}
\]

Its total is  

\[
\frac{29^{2}+1}{2}=421 .
\]

Choose any \(25\) white squares (they are never adjacent) and put
\(2\) coins on each of them instead of \(0\).  
Each such square now has two more coins than before, and its black
neighbours still have exactly one coin, so the difference on every edge
remains \(1\).

The new total is  

\[
421+25\cdot 2 = 421+50 = 471 .
\]

Thus a placement with 471 coins exists on a \(29\times 29\) board.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Maximum board size

No board larger than \(29\times 29\) can accommodate 471 coins under the
given condition, and a \(29\times 29\) board can. Therefore

\[
\boxed{\,n_{\max}=29\,}.
\]

