        [Turn 1 REASONING] (2590 chars)
        \[
f(g(x))-3g(f(x))\equiv k\qquad (\text{as a polynomial identity})
\]

Hence the polynomial  

\[
h(x)=f(g(x))-3g(f(x))-k
\]

has infinitely many real zeros; therefore \(h\) must be the zero polynomial.
Consequently  

\[
f(g(x))\equiv 3g(f(x))+k .\tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  The leading‑coefficient condition  

Let  

\[
\deg f=m\ge 2025,\qquad \deg g=n\ge 2025,
\]
\[
\operatorname{lc}(f)=a\ge 1000,\qquad \operatorname{lc}(g)=b\ge 1000 .
\]

Then  

\[
\deg\bigl(f\!\circ\! g\bigr)=mn=\deg\bigl(g\!\circ\! f\bigr).
\]

The leading term of \(f(g(x))\) is  

\[
a\bigl(bx^{\,n}\bigr)^{m}=a\,b^{\,m}\,x^{mn},
\]

while the leading term of \(3g(f(x))\) is  

\[
3b\bigl(ax^{\,m}\bigr)^{n}=3b\,a^{\,n}\,x^{mn}.
\]

For (1) to be a constant the coefficient of \(x^{mn}\) must vanish, i.e.

\[
a\,b^{\,m}=3b\,a^{\,n}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad b^{\,m-1}=3a^{\,n-1}. \tag{2}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Solving the Diophantine equation (2)

Write  

\[
a=3^{\alpha}r,\qquad b=3^{\beta}s,
\]

where \(3\nmid r,s\).  Substituting in (2) gives  

\[
3^{\beta(m-1)}s^{\,m-1}=3^{1+\alpha(n-1)}r^{\,n-1}.
\]

Hence  

\[
\begin{cases}
\beta(m-1)=1+\alpha(n-1),\\[2pt]
s^{\,m-1}=r^{\,n-1}.
\end{cases}\tag{3}
\]

Because \(r\) and \(s\) are not divisible by \(3\), the second equation forces
\(r\) and \(s\) to be perfect powers of a common integer \(t\):
\(r=t^{\ell},\;s=t^{k}\) with \(\ell,k\ge 1\).  Then  

\[
k(m-1)=\ell(n-1). \tag{4}
\]

Let \(d\) be a positive integer such that  

\[
m-1=\ell d,\qquad n-1=k d .
\]

Insert these expressions into the first equation of (3):

\[
\beta\ell d = 1 + \alpha k d\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
d\bigl(\beta\ell-\alpha k\bigr)=1. \tag{5}
\]

But \(d=m-1\ge 2024\).  Equation (5) can hold only when \(d=1\), which is
impossible.  Hence **no integers** \(a,b,m,n\) with  

\[
m,n\ge 2025,\qquad a,b\ge 1000
\]

satisfy the necessary condition (2).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Consequence for the original problem  

Since the leading‑coefficient condition cannot be met, the identity (1) cannot
hold for any pair of polynomials \(f,g\) of the required degrees and with
coefficients at least \(1000\).  Therefore there are **no** such polynomials,
and consequently there is **no** positive integer \(k\) for which the
statement of the problem is true.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
\[
\boxed{\text{No positive integer }k\text{ satisfies the condition.}}
\]

