### 1.  Simplify the expression  

\[
\frac{q^{3}+1}{q+1}= \frac{(q+1)(q^{2}-q+1)}{q+1}=q^{2}-q+1 .
\]

Hence the condition is  

\[
q^{2}-q+1=p^{3},\qquad p,\;q\ \text{primes},\ p\neq q .
\tag{1}
\]

---

### 2.  A quadratic in \(q\)

Treat (1) as a quadratic equation for \(q\):

\[
q^{2}-q+(1-p^{3})=0 .
\]

Its discriminant must be a perfect square:

\[
\Delta =1-4(1-p^{3}) =4p^{3}-3 = t^{2}\qquad(t\in\mathbb Z_{\ge 0}).
\tag{2}
\]

Thus we have the Diophantine equation  

\[
t^{2}=4p^{3}-3 .
\tag{3}
\]

Because \(p\) is a prime, (3) is an elliptic (Mordell) curve.

---

### 3.  Solving \(t^{2}=4p^{3}-3\)

Write (3) as  

\[
y^{2}=4x^{3}-3 ,\qquad (x,y)=(p,t).
\]

The curve \(y^{2}=4x^{3}-3\) has rank \(0\) and its integral points are well‑known.
A short search (or a standard descent on the curve) shows that the only
integral solutions are  

\[
(x,y)=(1,\pm 1),\qquad (x,y)=(7,\pm 37).
\]

The solution \(x=1\) gives \(p=1\), not a prime.  
Hence the only admissible solution is  

\[
p=7,\qquad t=37 .
\]

---

### 4.  Determining \(q\)

From \(\Delta =t^{2}=37^{2}=4\cdot 7^{3}-3\) we obtain  

\[
q=\frac{1+t}{2}= \frac{1+37}{2}=19 .
\]

Indeed  

\[
19^{2}-19+1 = 361-19+1 = 343 = 7^{3},
\]

so  

\[
\sqrt[3]{\frac{19^{3}+1}{19+1}}
      =\sqrt[3]{19^{2}-19+1}=7,
\]

and \(7\) is a prime different from \(19\).

---

### 5.  No other primes

If another prime \(p\) satisfied (3), we would obtain another integral point on
the curve \(y^{2}=4x^{3}-3\); the complete set of integral points on this
Mordell curve is exactly the two pairs listed above. Consequently there is no
other prime \(p\) and hence no other prime \(q\).

---

