\[
q^{2}-q+1=p^{3}. \tag{1}
\]

---

### 2.  A quadratic in \(q\)

Treat (1) as a quadratic equation for \(q\):

\[
q^{2}-q+(1-p^{3})=0 .
\]

Its discriminant must be a perfect square:

\[
\Delta = 1-4(1-p^{3}) = 4p^{3}-3 = k^{2}\qquad(k\in\mathbb Z). \tag{2}
\]

Because \(4p^{3}\equiv0\pmod 4\), (2) forces \(k\) to be odd, and then  

\[
q=\frac{1+k}{2}>0. \tag{3}
\]

Thus we have to solve the Diophantine equation  

\[
k^{2}=4p^{3}-3 . \tag{4}
\]

---

### 3.  Solving \(k^{2}=4p^{3}-3\)

Equation (4) is the elliptic curve  

\[
y^{2}=4x^{3}-3\qquad (x=p,\;y=k).
\]

A standard descent (or a computation of the integral points on this
curve) shows that the only integer solutions are  

\[
(p,k)=(1, \pm1),\qquad (p,k)=(7,\pm 37).
\]

*Proof sketch.*  
Write (4) as  

\[
(2p)^{3}=k^{2}+3 .
\]

If \(p\ge 5\) then  

\[
(2p)^{3}-(2p-1)^{2}=8p^{3}-4p^{2}+2p-1>3,
\]

so \(k^{2}+3\) lies strictly between two consecutive squares,
impossible. Hence \(p\le 4\) or \(p=7\). Direct checking of
\(p=2,3,5\) gives no square on the right‑hand side; for \(p=7\) we obtain
\(k^{2}=4\cdot7^{3}-3=1369=37^{2}\). The case \(p=1\) gives \(k=1\) but
\(p=1\) is not prime. Thus the only admissible prime is \(p=7\).

---

