Thus  

\[
q^{2}-q+1=p^{3}\tag{1}
\]

with \(p,q\) primes.

---

### 2.  Transform to an elliptic curve  

Treat (1) as a quadratic in \(q\):

\[
q^{2}-q+(1-p^{3})=0\Longrightarrow
q=\frac{1\pm\sqrt{\,4p^{3}-3\,}}{2}.
\]

Since \(q>0\), we must have  

\[
\sqrt{4p^{3}-3}=t\in\mathbb Z,\qquad 
q=\frac{1+t}{2}. \tag{2}
\]

Consequently  

\[
t^{2}=4p^{3}-3. \tag{3}
\]

Equation (3) is the Mordell (elliptic) curve  

\[
y^{2}=4x^{3}-3\qquad (x=p,\;y=t).
\]

---

### 3.  Integral points on the curve  

From (3) we need integer solutions \((p,t)\) with \(p\) prime.
A quick modular check shows that \(p\neq3\) and, for a prime
\(p\neq3\),

\[
4p^{3}-3\equiv p^{3}\pmod 3 .
\]

Since a square is \(0\) or \(1\pmod 3\), we must have
\(p^{3}\equiv1\pmod 3\); therefore  

\[
p\equiv1\pmod 3 .
\]

Now search for integer points on \(y^{2}=4x^{3}-3\) with \(x\equiv1\pmod 3\).
A direct computation (or standard results on Mordell curves) gives the
only integral point with \(x>1\):

\[
(p,t)=(7,37),
