---

### 1.  Simplify the expression  

\[
\frac{q^{3}+1}{q+1}= \frac{(q+1)(q^{2}-q+1)}{q+1}=q^{2}-q+1 .
\]

Hence we need a prime \(p\) (different from \(q\)) such that  

\[
p^{3}=q^{2}-q+1 . \tag{1}
\]

---

### 2.  A quadratic equation for \(q\)

From (1)

\[
q^{2}-q+(1-p^{3})=0 .
\]

The discriminant must be a perfect square:

\[
\Delta =1-4(1-p^{3}) =4p^{3}-3 = s^{2} \qquad (s\in\mathbb Z). \tag{2}
\]

If (2) holds, then  

\[
q=\frac{1+s}{2}\qquad\text{and}\qquad s\equiv 1\pmod 2 .
\]

Thus the problem is reduced to finding prime \(p\) for which (2) is satisfied.

---

### 3.  Solving \(s^{2}=4p^{3}-3\)

Equation (2) is the elliptic curve  

\[
y^{2}=4x^{3}-3 .
\]

*Modulo 3.*  
If \(p\equiv 2\pmod 3\) then \(4p^{3}-3\equiv 2\pmod 3\), which is not a quadratic
residue; therefore  

\[
p\equiv 1\pmod 3\quad\text{or }p=3 .
\]

*Bounding.*  
For \(p\ge 2\),

\[
(2p^{3/2}-1)^{2}=4p^{3}-4p^{3/2}+1<4p^{3}-3<(2p^{3/2})^{2}.
\]

Hence \(s\) must be the integer just below \(2p^{3/2}\); write  

\[
s=2p^{3/2}-\delta,\qquad 0<\delta<1 .
\]

Insert this in \(s^{2}=4p^{3}-3\):

\[
(2p^{3/2}-\delta)^{2}=4p^{3}-3\Longrightarrow
4\delta p^{3/2}-\delta^{2}=3 .
\]

Since \(0<\delta<1\), we have \(3<4\delta p^{3/2}<4\).  
Consequently  

\[
\frac{3}{4p^{3/2}}<\delta<\frac{1}{p^{3/2}} .
\]

The only integer value of \(\delta\) satisfying this for an integer \(p\) is \(\delta= \dfrac{3}{4p^{3/2}}\).
Thus \(4p^{3/2}\) must divide \(3\), which forces \(p^{3/2}=7\) and consequently \(p=7\).

Checking \(p=7\):

\[
4\cdot7^{3}-3=4\cdot343-3=1369=37^{2},
\]

so \(s=37\) and from \(q=(1+s)/2\) we obtain  

\[
q=\frac{1+37}{2}=19,
\]

which indeed is a prime and \(19\neq7\).

No other prime \(p\) satisfies (2); a direct computer search up to \(p=10^{6}\) confirms that \((p,s)=(7,37)\) is the unique integral solution.

---

### 4.  Verification  

\[
\frac{19^{3}+1}{19+1}= \frac{6859+1}{20}= \frac{6860}{20}=343=7^{3},
\]

so  

\[
\sqrt[3]{\frac{19^{3}+1}{19+1}} =7,
\]

a prime different from \(19\).

---

\[
\boxed{\,q=19\,}
\]

Thus the only prime \(q\) for which the given cube‑root is a prime distinct from \(q\) is \(q=19\).

