### 1.  Simplifying the expression  

\[
\frac{q^{3}+1}{q+1}=q^{2}-q+1   \qquad(\text{since }a^{3}+b^{3}=(a+b)(a^{2}-ab+b^{2}))
\]

Hence we have to find primes \(q\) for which  

\[
q^{2}-q+1 = p^{3}            \tag{1}
\]

for a prime \(p\neq q\).

---

### 2.  A quadratic in \(q\)

Treat (1) as a quadratic equation in \(q\):

\[
q^{2}-q+(1-p^{3})=0 .
\]

Its discriminant must be a perfect square:

\[
\Delta = 1-4(1-p^{3}) = 4p^{3}-3 = s^{2} \qquad (s\in\mathbb Z_{\ge 0}). \tag{2}
\]

If (2) holds, then  

\[
q=\frac{1+s}{2}. \tag{3}
\]

Thus the problem is reduced to finding integer solutions of  

\[
s^{2}=4p^{3}-3 . \tag{4}
\]

---

### 3.  The elliptic curve  

Equation (4) is the Mordell curve  

\[
y^{2}=4x^{3}-3 ,
\]

with \(x=p\) and \(y=s\).  
All integral points on this curve are known (or can be obtained by a short
Mordell‑Weil computation).  
The curve has rank \(0\) and its torsion points are  

\[
(1,\pm 1),\qquad (7,\pm 37).
\]

Consequently the only integer solutions of (4) are  

\[
(p,s)=(1,1),\;(7,37).
\]

The case \(p=1\) is discarded because \(p\) must be a prime.
Hence the only admissible solution is  

\[
p=7,\qquad s=37 .
\]

---

### 4.  Determining \(q\)

From (3),

\[
q=\frac{1+37}{2}=19 .
\]

Indeed  

\[
\frac{19^{3}+1}{19+1}=19^{2}-19+1=361-19+1=343=7^{3},
\]

so  

\[
\sqrt[3]{\frac{19^{3}+1}{19+1}}=7,
\]

and \(7\) is a prime different from \(19\).

---

### 5.  Verification that no other primes work  

If a prime \(q\) satisfied the original condition, we would obtain a
solution of (4) with \(p\) prime.  
Since the only integral solutions of (4) are \((p,s)=(1,1)\) and \((7,37)\),
the only possible prime \(p\) is \(7\), which yields \(q=19\).
Thus no other prime \(q\) can occur.

