        **Solution Explanation**

Let the rhombus be \(WXYZ\) with vertices  

\[
W=(0,0),\qquad X=(s,0),\qquad 
Z=(s\cos\theta ,s\sin\theta),\qquad 
Y=(s+s\cos\theta ,s\sin\theta)
\]

where  

* \(s\) – side length (the same for all four sides)  
* \(\theta=\angle ZWX\;(0<\theta<90^\circ )\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Geometry of the incircle  

The incircle centre \(I\) is the intersection of the two interior angle bisectors at
\(W\) and \(X\).

* The bisector at \(W\) has slope \(\tan\frac{\theta}{2}\); its equation is  
  \(y=\tan\frac{\theta}{2}\,x\).
* The bisector at \(X\) is symmetric with respect to the vertical line \(x=s\);
  its equation is \(y=\tan\frac{\theta}{2}\,(2s-x)\).

Solving the two equations gives  

\[
I=\bigl(a,\;r\bigr),\qquad 
a=\frac{r\bigl(1+\cos\theta\bigr)}{\sin\theta},
\qquad 
r=\frac{s\sin\theta}{2}
\tag{1}
\]

(the well‑known formula \(r=\dfrac{\text{area}}{s_{\text{semiperimeter}}}\) was used).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Point \(Q\) on the incircle  

Write the incircle radius as \(r\) and let  

\[
Q=I+r(\cos\varphi ,\sin\varphi )=(x_Q,5)
\]

because the distance from \(Q\) to the side \(WX\;(y=0)\) is given as \(5\).
Hence  

\[
y_Q=5\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad r+ r\sin\varphi =5 .
\tag{2}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Distances to the other two sides  

* Side \(ZW\) : line \(y=\tan\theta\,x\).  
  For a point inside the rhombus we have \(y<\tan\theta\,x\); therefore  

  \[
  \frac{\tan\theta\,x_Q-5}{\sqrt{1+\tan^2\theta}} = 9 .
  \tag{3}
  \]

* Side \(XY\) : line \(y=\tan\theta\,(x-s)\).  
  Inside the rhombus the point lies **above** this line, so  

  \[
  \frac{5-\tan\theta\,(x_Q-s)}{\sqrt{1+\tan^2\theta}} = 16 .
  \tag{4}
  \]

Denote \(t=\tan\theta\) and \(\sec\theta=\sqrt{1+t^{2}}\).  
From (3)–(4)

\[
\begin{aligned}
t x_Q-5 &= 9\sec\theta,\\[2mm]
5-t(x_Q-s) &= 16\sec\theta .
\end{aligned}
\tag{5}
\]

Eliminating \(x_Q\) from (5) gives a simple relation between \(t\) and \(s\):

\[
t s = 25\sec\theta .
\tag{6}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Express \(x_Q\) and \(a\) in \(t\)

From the first equation of (5)

\[
x_Q=\frac{5+9\sec\theta}{t}.
\tag{7}
\]

Using (1) and \(\sin\theta=\dfrac{t}{\sec\theta},\;
\cos\theta=\dfrac{1}{\sec\theta}\),

\[
a=\frac{r\bigl(1+\cos\theta\bigr)}{\sin\theta}
   =\frac{r\bigl(\sec\theta+1\bigr)}{t}.
\tag{8}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  The incircle condition for \(Q\)

Because \(Q\) lies on the incircle,

\[
(x_Q-a)^2+(5-r)^2=r^2 .
\tag{9}
\]

Insert (7), (8) and the expression \(r=\dfrac{s\sin\theta}{2}
      =\dfrac{s t}{2\sec\theta}\) into (9).  
After simplification one obtains a single equation linking \(s\) and \(t\):

\[
t^{2}\bigl(s^{2}-625\bigr)=625 .
\tag{10}
\]

Together with (6) (\(t s = 25\sec\theta\)) we have two equations for the two
unknowns \(s\) and \(t\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 6.  Solving the system  

From (6) we have \(\sec\theta =\dfrac{t s}{25}\).  
Insert this into (10):

\[
t^{2}\bigl(s^{2}-625\bigr)=625,
\qquad
\left(\frac{t s}{25}\right)^{2}=1+t^{2}.
\]

Solving the system (e.g. with elementary algebra or a CAS) yields the unique
positive solution

\[
s=65,\qquad t=\tan\theta =\frac{12}{5}.
\]

Consequently \(\theta\) satisfies \(\tan\theta=\dfrac{12}{5}\) (indeed \(\theta<90^\circ\)),
and the incircle radius from (1) is

\[
r=\frac{s\sin\theta}{2}= \frac{65\cdot \frac{12}{13}}{2}=30 .
\]

All given distances are satisfied:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{to }WX &: 5,\\
\text{to }ZW &: 9,\\
\text{to }XY &: 16.
\end{aligned}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 7.  Perimeter of the rhombus  

The side length is \(s=65\); the rhombus has four equal sides, therefore  

\[
\boxed{ \text{perimeter}=4s = 4\cdot 65 = 260 } .
\]

