For each \(i\) we have the lower bound \(a_{i+1}\ge S_i\).  
If we increase \(a_{i+1}\) above this bound, the term \(\dfrac{a_i}{a_{i+1}}\) gets
smaller, while the later terms are not increased enough to compensate.
Thus the sum is maximised when every inequality is an equality:

\[
a_{i+1}=S_i\qquad (i=1,\dots ,2023).
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Determining the sequence

Set \(a_1>0\).  
From \(a_2=S_1=a_1\) we obtain \(a_2=a_1\).

Inductively, if \(a_k=2^{k-2}a_1\;(k\ge2)\) then  

\[
S_{k}=a_1+\sum_{j=2}^{k}2^{j-2}a_1
      =a_1\bigl(1+1+2+\dots+2^{k-2}\bigr)=2^{k-1}a_1,
\]
so \(a_{k+1}=S_k=2^{k-1}a_1\).  
Hence  

\[
\boxed{a_1>0,\qquad a_i=2^{\,i-2}a_1\;(i\ge2)} .
\]

Consequently  

\[
\frac{a_i}{a_{i+1}}=
\begin{cases}
1, & i=1,\\[2mm]
\dfrac{2^{i-2}a_1}{2^{i-1}a_1}= \dfrac12, & i\ge2 .
