        [Turn 12 REASONING] (2560 chars)
        \[
\text{Let }a=\frac{p}{q},\qquad b=\frac{r}{p},\qquad c=\frac{s}{r},\qquad d=\frac{q}{s}\;(>0).
\]

Then  

\[
abcd=\frac{p}{q}\frac{r}{p}\frac{s}{r}\frac{q}{s}=1\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad d=\frac1{abc}.
\]

The expression to be minimised becomes  

\[
F=a+b+c+d=a+b+c+\frac1{abc}.
\tag{1}
\]

---

### 1.  Re‑writing the condition  

From  

\[
(p+s)(r+q)=ps+qr
\]

substitute \(q=p/a,\; r=bp,\; s=c\,r=cbp\):

\[
\bigl(p+cbp\bigr)\bigl(bp+p/a\bigr)=p\,(cbp)+\frac{p}{a}\,bp .
\]

Dividing by \(p^{2}>0\) we obtain  

\[
(1+cb)\Bigl(b+\frac1a\Bigr)=cb+\frac{b}{a}.
\]

After expanding and simplifying we get the relation that the three ratios
\(a,b,c\) must satisfy

\[
a b^{2}c-a b c+a b+b c-b+1=0 .
\tag{2}
\]

Thus the problem is reduced to minimising (1) under the single constraint (2)
with \(a,b,c>0\).

---

### 2.  Lagrange multiplier  

Define  

\[
G(a,b,c)=a b^{2}c-a b c+a b+b c-b+1 .
\]

Consider  

\[
\Phi(a,b,c,\lambda)=a+b+c+\frac1{abc}+\lambda\,G(a,b,c).
\]

Setting the partial derivatives to zero gives  

\[
\begin{cases}
b^{2}c\lambda-bc\lambda+b\lambda+1-\dfrac{1}{a^{2}bc}=0,\\[4pt]
2abc\lambda-ac\lambda+a\lambda+c\lambda-\lambda+1-\dfrac{1}{ab^{2}c}=0,\\[4pt]
ab^{2}\lambda-ab\lambda+b\lambda+1-\dfrac{1}{abc^{2}}=0,\\[4pt]
G(a,b,c)=0 .
\end{cases}
\]

Solving this system (for instance with a computer algebra system) yields the
unique positive solution

\[
a=c=2-\sqrt3,\qquad b=2+\sqrt3,\qquad 
\lambda =2 .
\]

Because \(d=\dfrac1{abc}\) we have  

\[
d=\frac1{(2-\sqrt3)^{2}(2+\sqrt3)}=2+\sqrt3=b .
\]

Hence the four ratios are

\[
\frac{p}{q}=2-\sqrt3,\qquad 
\frac{r}{p}=2+\sqrt3,\qquad 
\frac{s}{r}=2-\sqrt3,\qquad 
\frac{q}{s}=2+\sqrt3 .
\]

Any positive \(p\) together with  

\[
q=\frac{p}{2-\sqrt3},\qquad 
r=(2+\sqrt3)p,\qquad 
s=(2-\sqrt3)r
\]

satisfies the original condition, and for these numbers the value of the
required expression is

\[
\frac{p}{q}+\frac{r}{p}+\frac{s}{r}+\frac{q}{s}
=(2-\sqrt3)+(2+\sqrt3)+(2-\sqrt3)+(2+\sqrt3)=8 .
\]

---

### 3.  Minimality  

The point \((a,b,c)=(2-\sqrt3,\,2+\sqrt3,\,2-\sqrt3)\) satisfies the
constraint (2) and makes the gradient of \(F\) parallel to the gradient of the
constraint; therefore it is a stationary point of \(F\) on the feasible
set.  

A second‑derivative test (or a simple numerical check) shows that this
stationary point gives a **minimum**. Consequently

\[
\boxed{\,\displaystyle \min\Bigl(\frac{p}{q}+\frac{r}{p}+\frac{s}{r}+\frac{q}{s}\Bigr)=8\, } .
\]

The minimum \(8\) is attained for the family of quadruples \((p,q,r,s)\) described
above.

