\text{Let }A=\frac{p}{q},\;B=\frac{r}{p},\;C=\frac{s}{r},\;D=\frac{q}{s}\qquad (A,B,C,D>0).
\]

Then  

\[
ABCD=\frac{p}{q}\cdot\frac{r}{p}\cdot\frac{s}{r}\cdot\frac{q}{s}=1,
\qquad 
\frac{p}{q}+ \frac{r}{p}+ \frac{s}{r}+ \frac{q}{s}=A+B+C+D .
\tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  The condition in the new variables  

Because the given equation is homogeneous of degree two we may divide it
by \(q^{2}\) and write \(p=Aq,\;r=ABq,\;s=ABCq\).  
Substituting these expressions into  

\[
(p+s)(r+q)=ps+qr
\]

and cancelling the factor \(q^{2}>0\) we obtain  

\[
A\bigl(1+BC\bigr)\bigl(AB+1\bigr)=A^{2}BC+AB .
\]

Dividing by \(A\;(>0)\) and simplifying gives  

\[
A B^{2}C-AB C+AB+BC-B+1=0 .
\tag{2}
\]

Thus the four numbers \(A,B,C,D\;(=1/(ABC))\) satisfy (2) and (1).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Reducing to three variables  

From (2) we can solve for \(C\):

\[
C=\frac{B-1}{AB^{2}-AB+1}.
\tag{3}
\]

Insert (3) into the product condition \(AB C D =1\) (i.e. \(D=1/(ABC)\)).
After simplification we obtain a relation that involves only \(A\) and \(B\):

\[
A\bigl(2B-1\bigr)=B-2 .
\tag{4}
\]

Hence  

\[
A=\frac{B-2}{2B-1},\qquad C=\frac1B,\qquad D=\frac1A .
\]

Because all variables are positive, (4) forces \(B>2\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  The expression to minimise  

Using the above expressions in (1) we get a function of a single variable
\(B>2\):

\[
\begin{aligned}
S(B)&=A+B+C+D \\
    &=\frac{B-2}{2B-1}+B+\frac1B+\frac{2B-1}{B-2}\\[2mm]
    &=\frac{2\bigl(B^{4}-2B^{2}+1\bigr)}
            {B\bigl(2B^{2}-5B+2\bigr)} .
\end{aligned}
\tag{5}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Minimising \(S(B)\)

Differentiate (5).  After factorisation we obtain  

\[
S'(B)=\frac{4\,(B-1)(B+1)(B^{2}-4B+1)(B^{2}-B+1)}
           {B^{2}(B-2)^{2}(2B-1)^{2}} .
\]

For \(B>2\) the only zero of the numerator is given by  

\[
B^{2}-4B+1=0\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
B=2+\sqrt3 .
\]

Since \(S'(B)>0\) for \(B>2+\sqrt3\) and \(S'(B)<0\) for \(2<B<2+\sqrt3\),
\(B=2+\sqrt3\) gives the global minimum of \(S\) on the admissible
interval.

Substituting \(B=2+\sqrt3\) into (4) gives  

\[
A=C=2-\sqrt3,\qquad D=\frac1A=2+\sqrt3 .
\]

Finally, from (5)

\[
S_{\min}=A+B+C+D
        =\bigl(2-\sqrt3\bigr)+(2+\sqrt3)+(2-\sqrt3)+(2+\sqrt3)=8 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Returning to the original variables  

The equalities \(A=C\) and \(B=D\) mean  

\[
\frac{p}{q}=\frac{s}{r},\qquad
\frac{r}{p}=\frac{q}{s},
\]

i.e. \(p=s\) and \(q=r\).  
