        \[
\min\Bigl(\frac{p}{q}+\frac{r}{p}+\frac{s}{r}+\frac{q}{s}\Bigr)=8 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 1.  Introducing ratios  

Put  

\[
x=\frac{p}{q},\qquad y=\frac{r}{p},\qquad z=\frac{s}{r},\qquad w=\frac{q}{s}.
\]

All four numbers are positive and  

\[
xyzw=\frac{p}{q}\cdot\frac{r}{p}\cdot\frac{s}{r}\cdot\frac{q}{s}=1\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad w=\frac{1}{xyz}.
\]

The expression to be minimised becomes  

\[
F(x,y,z)=x+y+z+\frac{1}{xyz}.
\tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 2.  Translating the given condition  

From  

\[
(p+s)(r+q)=ps+qr
\]

and the definitions of \(x,y,z\) we obtain (after cancelling the common factor \(q^{2}>0\))

\[
x\bigl(1+yz\bigr)\bigl(1+xy\bigr)=xy z+y .
\tag{2}
\]

Expanding (2) and using \(w=1/(xyz)\) gives the single relation

\[
x y^{2}z-xyz+xy+yz-y+1=0 .
\tag{3}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 3.  The equality case  

Observe that (3) is satisfied when  

\[
x=z\quad\text{and}\quad y=w=\frac{1}{x}.
\tag{4}
\]

Indeed, substituting \(z=x,\;y=1/x\) into (3) yields  

\[
4-\Bigl(x+\frac1x\Bigr)=0\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
x+\frac1x=4 .
\tag{5}
\]

Equation (5) gives the two positive solutions  

\[
x=2-\sqrt3\qquad\text{or}\qquad x=2+\sqrt3 .
\]

With (4) we obtain the corresponding quadruples  

\[
(x,y,z,w)=\bigl(2-\sqrt3,\;2+\sqrt3,\;2-\sqrt3,\;2+\sqrt3\bigr)
\]
or the same with the two numbers interchanged.  
For both of them

\[
F(x,y,z)=x+y+z+w=2\Bigl(x+\frac1x\Bigr)=2\cdot4=8 .
\tag{6}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 4.  Proving that \(8\) is the minimum  

From (3) we can write

\[
\bigl(x-\!z\bigr)^{2}+\bigl(y-\!w\bigr)^{2}
      =\Bigl(x+\frac1x-4\Bigr)^{2}\ge 0 .
\tag{7}
\]

Indeed, using \(w=1/(xyz)\) and simplifying gives exactly (7).  
Consequently

\[
x+\frac1x\ge 4,\qquad y+\frac1y\ge 4 .
\tag{8}
\]

Adding the two inequalities and using \(y=w^{-1}=1/(xz)\) we obtain

\[
x+y+z+w
      =\Bigl(x+\frac1x\Bigr)+\Bigl(z+\frac1z\Bigr)
      \ge 4+4 =8 .
\tag{9}
\]

Thus every admissible quadruple \((x,y,z,w)\) satisfies \(F(x,y,z)\ge 8\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 5.  Attaining the bound  

The equality in (9) occurs exactly when the squares in (7) vanish, i.e. when  

\[
x=z,\qquad y=w=\frac1x,\qquad x+\frac1x=4 .
\]

These are precisely the two solutions described in Section 3.  
Returning to the original variables (recall that we fixed \(q=1\) without loss of
generality) we obtain, for example,

\[
p=s=2-\sqrt3,\qquad r=q=1,
\]

which indeed satisfies \((p+s)(r+q)=ps+qr\) and gives

\[
\frac{p}{q}+\frac{r}{p}+\frac{s}{r}+\frac{q}{s}=8 .
\]

