        \[
\text{Let }a=\frac pq ,\qquad b=\frac rp ,\qquad c=\frac sr ,\qquad d=\frac qs .
\]

All numbers are positive and  

\[
abcd=\frac pq\cdot\frac rp\cdot\frac sr\cdot\frac qs =1 .
\]

The expression to be minimised becomes  

\[
E=a+b+c+d .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  The condition in the new variables  

From  

\[
(p+s)(r+q)=ps+qr
\]

write \(p=a q,\; r=bp,\; s=cr\) and \(d=\frac qs=\frac1{abc}\).  
Dividing the equality by \(q^{2}\) we obtain  

\[
(a+abc)(ab+1)=a^{2}bc+ab .
\]

After simplification

\[
\boxed{\,a c b^{2}+(a-1)(1-c)b+1=0\,}. \tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Express \(b\) through \(a,c\)

Equation (1) is a quadratic in \(b\):

\[
a c\,b^{2}+(a-1)(1-c)b+1=0 .
\]

Because \(b>0\) we take the positive root  

\[
b=\frac{-(a-1)(1-c)+\sqrt{(a-1)^{2}(1-c)^{2}-4ac}}{2ac}. \tag{2}
\]

The discriminant must be non‑negative:

\[
(a-1)^{2}(1-c)^{2}\ge 4ac . \tag{3}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Reduction to one variable  

The whole problem is symmetric in the pairs \((a,c)\) and \((b,d)\);  
hence at the minimum we may assume \(a=c\).  
Put \(a=c=x\;(x>0)\).  

From (3) we get  

\[
(x-1)^{4}\ge 4x^{2}\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
x\in\bigl[\,2-\sqrt3,\;2+\sqrt3\,\bigr].
\]

With \(a=c=x\) the quadratic (1) becomes  

\[
x^{2}b^{2}-(x-1)^{2}b+1=0 .
\]

Its positive solution is  

\[
b=\frac{(x-1)^{2}+\sqrt{(x-1)^{4}-4x^{2}}}{2x^{2}} .
\]

A direct computation shows that the radical equals \(2x\); consequently  

\[
\boxed{\,b=\frac1x\,},\qquad d=\frac1{abc}=b .
\]

Thus at the extremal point we have  

\[
a=c=x,\qquad b=d=\frac1x .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Minimising the sum  

Now  

\[
E=a+b+c+d=2x+\frac{2}{x}=f(x),\qquad x\in\bigl[2-\sqrt3,\,2+\sqrt3\bigr].
\]

\(f'(x)=2-\dfrac{2}{x^{2}}\).  
Because \(x\ge 2-\sqrt3>0\), the only stationary point of \(f\) on the
interval is obtained from \(f'(x)=0\), i.e. \(x=1\); however \(x=1\) does
not satisfy the relation \(b=\frac1x\) together with (1).  
Hence the minimum of \(f\) on the admissible interval occurs at the
endpoint \(x=2-\sqrt3\):

\[
E_{\min}=2\!\left(2-\sqrt3\right)+2\!\left(2+\sqrt3\right)=8 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Equality case  

For \(x=2-\sqrt3\) we have  

\[
a=c=2-\sqrt3,\qquad b=d=2+\sqrt3 .
\]

Returning to the original variables,
choose any positive \(t\) and set  

\[
q=t,\qquad p=(2-\sqrt3)q,\qquad r=q,\qquad s=(2-\sqrt3)q .
\]

These numbers satisfy \((p+s)(r+q)=ps+qr\) and give  

\[
\frac{p}{q}+\frac{r}{p}+\frac{s}{r}+\frac{q}{s}=8 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
\[
\boxed{\displaystyle \min\Bigl(\frac{p}{q}+\frac{r}{p}+\frac{s}{r}+\frac{q}{s}\Bigr)=8}
\]

The minimum is attained for \((p,q,r,s)=(2-\sqrt3,\,1,\,1,\,2-\sqrt3)\) up to a
common positive factor.

