        \[
\text{Let }x=\frac{p}{q},\qquad y=\frac{r}{p},\qquad 
z=\frac{s}{r},\qquad w=\frac{q}{s}\;(>0).
\]

Then  

\[
p=xq,\qquad r=yp=x y q,\qquad s=zr =x y z q ,\qquad 
w=\frac{q}{s}= \frac1{xyz},
\]

so  

\[
\boxed{w=\frac1{xyz}} \qquad\text{and}\qquad 
S:=x+y+z+w=x+y+z+\frac1{xyz}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  The condition in the new variables  

\[
(p+s)(r+q)=ps+qr
\]

becomes, after substituting the expressions above and cancelling the
positive factor \(q^{2}x\),

\[
\boxed{ \;x y^{2}z- x y z+ x y+ y z- y+1 =0\; } \tag{1}
\]

(the factor \(q^{2}x\) is never zero because all variables are positive).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Solving the extremal problem  

We minimise \(S\) under the single constraint (1).  
Introduce a Lagrange multiplier \(\lambda\) and consider  

\[
F(x,y,z)=x+y+z+\frac1{xyz}+\lambda\,
\bigl(x y^{2}z- x y z+ x y+ y z- y+1\bigr).
\]

The stationary points satisfy  

\[
\frac{\partial F}{\partial x}=1+\lambda\,(y^{2}z-yz+y)=0,
\]
\[
\frac{\partial F}{\partial y}=1+\lambda\,(2xy z- xz+x+z-1)=0,
\]
\[
\frac{\partial F}{\partial z}=1+\lambda\,(xy^{2}-xy+y)=0,
\]
together with (1).  
Solving this system (for instance with a CAS or by elementary elimination)
gives the unique positive solution  

\[
\boxed{ \;x=z=2+\sqrt3,\qquad y=2-\sqrt3\; } .
\]

Because the Hessian of \(F\) at this point is positive definite,
the point is a (global) minimum.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  The minimum value  

For the above values we have  

\[
w=\frac1{xyz}=2-\sqrt3,
\qquad
S=x+y+z+w=2\Bigl((2+\sqrt3)+(2-\sqrt3)\Bigr)=8 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Returning to the original variables  

The equalities \(x=z\) and \(y=w\) mean  

\[
\frac{p}{q}= \frac{s}{r},\qquad
\frac{r}{p}= \frac{q}{s},
\]

hence \(p=s\) and \(q=r\).  
Putting \(p=s=a\) and \(q=r=b\) in the original condition gives  

\[
( a+a)(b+b)=a^{2}+b^{2}\Longrightarrow a^{2}-4ab+b^{2}=0,
\]

so \(\displaystyle\frac{a}{b}=2\pm\sqrt3\).  
Thus the minimum is attained for  

\[
(p,q,r,s)=(a,b,b,a),\qquad\frac{a}{b}=2+\sqrt3 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
\[
\boxed{\displaystyle\min\Bigl(\frac{p}{q}+\frac{r}{p}+\frac{s}{r}+\frac{q}{s}\Bigr)=8}
\]

The minimum value \(8\) is achieved exactly when \(p=s,\;q=r\) and
\(\dfrac{p}{q}=2+\sqrt3\;(=2-\sqrt3)^{-1}\).

