        \[
\text{Let }a=\frac{p}{q},\qquad b=\frac{r}{p},\qquad c=\frac{s}{r},\qquad d=\frac{q}{s}.
\]

All four numbers are positive and  

\[
abcd=\frac{p}{q}\cdot\frac{r}{p}\cdot\frac{s}{r}\cdot\frac{q}{s}=1 .
\tag{1}
\]

---

### 1.  The condition in the new variables  

\[
(p+s)(r+q)=ps+qr .
\]

Writing \(p=a q,\; r=b p=abq,\; s=c r=abcq\) and using (1) we obtain after cancelling the common factor
\(aq^{2}>0\)

\[
(1+cb)(1+ba)=b(ac+1). \tag{2}
\]

---

### 2.  A first AM–GM estimate  

From (2) and the positivity of the variables

\[
(1+cb)(1+ba)\ge 4\sqrt{cb\;ba}=4b\sqrt{ac}.
\]

Hence  

\[
4b\sqrt{ac}\le b(ac+1)\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
4\sqrt{ac}\le ac+1 .
\]

Put  

\[
t=\sqrt{ac}>0 .
\]

Then  

\[
t^{2}-4t+1\ge 0\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
t\le 2-\sqrt3\ \ \text{or}\ \ t\ge 2+\sqrt3 . \tag{3}
\]

---

### 3.  Lower bound for the required sum  

From (1) we have \(bd=\dfrac1{ac}=t^{-2}\).  
Applying AM–GM to the pairs \((a,c)\) and \((b,d)\),

\[
a+c\ge 2t,\qquad b+d\ge 2\frac1t .
\]

Consequently  

\[
S:=a+b+c+d\ge 2t+\frac{2}{t}=f(t),\qquad t>0 .
\tag{4}
\]

The function \(f(t)=2t+2/t\) is decreasing on \((0,1]\) and increasing on \([1,\infty)\); its minimum on the admissible
intervals given by (3) is attained at the endpoints \(t=2-\sqrt3\) or \(t=2+\sqrt3\).  
Evaluating \(f\) there gives

\[
f(2\pm\sqrt3)=2(2\pm\sqrt3)+\frac{2}{2\pm\sqrt3}
          =4\mp2\sqrt3+4\pm2\sqrt3=8 .
\]

Thus  

\[
\boxed{\,S\ge 8\,}.
\]

---

### 4.  When does equality occur?  

Equality in the AM–GM steps of (2) and of (4) requires  

\[
a=c,\qquad b=d .
\]

With \(a=c=t\) and \(b=d=1/t\) the condition (2) becomes

\[
(1+t\cdot\frac1t)(1+\frac1t\cdot t)=\frac1t\,(t^{2}+1)
\Longrightarrow 4=t+\frac1t .
\]

Hence \(t\) satisfies \(t^{2}-4t+1=0\), i.e.

\[
t=2\pm\sqrt3 .
\]

Therefore  

\[
a=c=2\pm\sqrt3,\qquad b=d=2\mp\sqrt3 .
\]

Returning to the original variables, this means  

\[
p=s,\qquad q=r,\qquad \frac{p}{q}=2\pm\sqrt3 .
\]

For such a choice the given condition is fulfilled and  

\[
\frac{p}{q}+\frac{r}{p}+\frac{s}{r}+\frac{q}{s}=a+b+c+d=8 .
\]

---

\[
\boxed{\displaystyle \min\Bigl(\frac{p}{q}+\frac{r}{p}+\frac{s}{r}+\frac{q}{s}\Bigr)=8}
\] 

The minimum is attained, for instance, at  

\[
p=s,\; q=r,\; \frac{p}{q}=2+\sqrt3\;( \text{or }2-\sqrt3).
\]

