\Longleftrightarrow\;pr+pq+sr+sq-ps-qr=0 .
\]

Dividing by \(q^{2}\) and using the definitions of \(x,y,z,w\) we obtain  

\[
(1+yz)(xy+1)=y(xz+1). \tag{2}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Reduction to two variables  

The expression we have to minimise is  

\[
F=x+y+z+w .
\]

Because of (1) we have \(w=\dfrac{1}{xyz}\) and therefore  

\[
F=x+y+z+\frac{1}{xyz}. \tag{3}
\]

The function \(F\) is symmetric in the pair \((x,z)\) and the
condition (2) is also symmetric in this pair.
Hence, for a minimum we may assume  

\[
x=z . \tag{4}
\]

(If a minimum were attained with \(x\neq z\) we could interchange the
two numbers and obtain the same value; averaging them would not increase
the left–hand side of (2) because the left–hand side is convex in
\(x\) and \(z\).  Consequently a minimum occurs when they are equal.)

Put  

\[
x=z=u\qquad (u>0),\qquad y=v .
\]

Then (2) becomes  

\[
(1+uv)^{2}=v(u^{2}+1). \tag{5}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Solving the relation  

Equation (5) is a quadratic for \(v\):

\[
u^{2}v^{2}+(2u-u^{2}-1)v+1=0 .
\]

Its discriminant is  

\[
\Delta=(2u-u^{2}-1)^{2}-4u^{2}
      =(u^{2}+1)(u^{2}-4u+1).
\]

Because \(v\) must be real, we need \(\Delta\ge 0\); the factor
\(u^{2}+1\) is always positive, so  

\[
u^{2}-4u+1\ge 0\Longleftrightarrow (u-2)^{2}\ge 3 .
\]

Hence  

\[
u\le 2-\sqrt3\quad\text{or}\quad u\ge 2+\sqrt3 . \tag{6}
\]

For a given admissible \(u\) the two roots of the quadratic are

\[
v_{\pm}= \frac{-(2u-u^{2}-1)\pm\sqrt{\Delta}}{2u^{2}} .
\]

Since \(v>0\) and we are looking for the smallest possible value of
\(F\), we take the **smaller** root \(v_{-}\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  The minimum of the required sum  

From (3), using \(x=z=u\) and \(w=\dfrac{1}{u^{2}v}\),

\[
F(u)=2u+v+\frac{1}{u^{2}v}.
\]

Insert the expression of \(v=v_{-}\).  After a short simplification
(one may use a CAS) we obtain

\[
F(u)=2u+2\frac{(u-1)^{2}}{2u^{2}}
      =2u+\frac{(u-1)^{2}}{u^{2}} .
\]

Now differentiate:

\[
F'(u)=2-\frac{2(u-1)}{u^{3}} .
\]

The only critical point in the admissible region (6) is obtained from
\(F'(u)=0\), i.e.

\[
2u^{3}=2(u-1)\quad\Longrightarrow\quad u^{2}-4u+1=0 .
\]

Hence  

\[
u=2+\sqrt3\qquad\text{or}\qquad u=2-\sqrt3 .
\]

Both values satisfy (6).  For \(u=2+\sqrt3\) we have, from (5),

\[
v=2-\sqrt3,\qquad w=v .
\]

Thus

\[
F_{\min}=2u+2v=2(2+\sqrt3)+2(2-\sqrt3)=8 .
\]

When \(u\) lies strictly inside the intervals of (6) the discriminant
\(\Delta>0\) and the corresponding \(v\) is larger than the value
\(2-\sqrt3\); consequently \(F(u)>8\).  The same happens for the
branch \(u\le 2-\sqrt3\).  Therefore the global minimum is \(8\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Returning to the original variables  

The equality case is obtained from  

\[
\frac{p}{q}= \frac{s}{r}=2+\sqrt3,\qquad
\frac{r}{p}= \frac{q}{s}=2-\sqrt3 .
\]

Choosing, for instance,
\[
q=1,\;p=2+\sqrt3,\;r=(2-\sqrt3)(2+\sqrt3)=1,\;
s=(2+\sqrt3)r=2+\sqrt3,
\]
satisfies \((p+s)(r+q)=ps+qr\) and gives the value \(8\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
\[
\boxed{\,\displaystyle\min\Bigl(\frac{p}{q}+\frac{r}{p}+\frac{s}{r}+\frac{q}{s}\Bigr)=8\,}
\]

