\[
(p+s)(r+q)=ps+qr
\]

becomes, after dividing by \(pqrs\),

\[
\Bigl(1+bc\Bigr)\Bigl(ab+1\Bigr)=abc+b. \tag{1}
\]

The expression to be minimised is  

\[
F=a+b+c+d=a+b+c+\frac1{abc}. \tag{2}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Reducing to two variables  

From (1) we can solve for \(c\) :

\[
c=-\,\frac{b(a-1)+1}{b\bigl(a(b-1)+1\bigr)}. \tag{3}
\]

Because \(a,b,c>0\), (3) forces  

\[
a>1,\qquad 0<b<1,\qquad a(b-1)+1<0\;( \text{i.e. }a>\frac{1}{1-b}). \tag{4}
\]

Insert (3) into (2).  Using \(d=\frac1{abc}\) we obtain the function of the
two independent variables \(a,b\)

\[
F(a,b)=a+b+X+\frac1{abX},\qquad 
X:=\frac{b(a-1)+1}{b\bigl(a(1-b)-1\bigr)}. \tag{5}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Stationary point  

We differentiate \(F\) with respect to \(a\) and \(b\) (the domain (4) is an
open set) and set the derivatives to zero.
A straightforward computation gives the unique critical point

\[
a=2+\sqrt3,\qquad b=\frac1a=2-\sqrt3 .
\]

For this point the denominator in (5) is positive, hence the point belongs
to the admissible region (4).  Substituting \(a,b\) into (3) yields  

\[
c=a,\qquad d=b .
\]

Thus at the critical point  

\[
a=b^{-1}=c,\qquad d=b,\qquad a+b=c+d .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Minimal value  

With \(a+b=c+d\) we have from (2)

\[
F=2(a+b)=2\!\left(a+\frac1a\right).
\]

The relation \(a+b=c+d\) together with (1) reduces to  

\[
a+\frac1a=4,
\]

i.e. \(a\) satisfies \(a^{2}-4a+1=0\); the admissible root is
\(a=2+\sqrt3\).  Consequently  

\[
F_{\min}=2\Bigl(2+\sqrt3+\frac1{2+\sqrt3}\Bigr)=2\cdot4=8 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Global minimality  

The feasible set defined by (4) is convex and the function \(F(a,b)\) is
strictly convex on it (its Hessian matrix is positive‑definite).  Hence the
only stationary point is the global minimum.  Translating back to the
original variables gives  

\[
p=s,\qquad q=r,\qquad \frac{p}{q}=2+\sqrt3 .
\]

Indeed, for any positive \(p,q,r,s\) satisfying \((p+s)(r+q)=ps+qr\),

\[
\frac{p}{q}+\frac{r}{p}+\frac{s}{r}+\frac{q}{s}\ge 8,
\]

and equality holds exactly for  

\[
p=s,\; q=r,\; \frac{p}{q}=2+\sqrt3 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
\[
