        \[
a_{n+1}=2^{n}-7a_n ,\qquad n=0,1,2,\dots 
\]

---

### 1.  Solve the recurrence  

Write it as  

\[
a_{n+1}+7a_n = 2^{n}.
\]

The homogeneous part gives  

\[
a^{(h)}_n = C(-7)^{\,n}.
\]

For a particular solution try \(a_n = p\,2^{n}\):

\[
p2^{n+1}=2^{n}-7p2^{n}\Longrightarrow 2p=1-7p\Longrightarrow p=\frac1{9}.
\]

Hence  

\[
a_n = C(-7)^{\,n}+\frac{1}{9}2^{n}.
\]

Let  

\[
C = a_0-\frac19 .
\]

Thus  

\[
\boxed{\,a_n =\bigl(a_0-\tfrac19\bigr)(-7)^{\,n}+ \frac19\,2^{n}\,}.
\]

---

### 2.  Impose the monotonicity condition  

We need  

\[
a_{n+1}>a_n \qquad\text{for all } n\ge 1 .
\]

Using the closed form,

\[
\begin{aligned}
a_{n+1}-a_n &=
\bigl(a_0-\tfrac19\bigr)\bigl[(-7)^{n+1}-(-7)^{n}\bigr]
        +\frac19\bigl(2^{n+1}-2^{n}\bigr)\\[2mm]
&= -8\bigl(a_0-\tfrac19\bigr)(-7)^{n}+ \frac19\,2^{n}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Set \(D=a_0-\frac19\).  
The inequality \(a_{n+1}>a_n\) becomes  

\[
\frac19\,2^{n}>8D(-7)^{n}
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
D(-1)^{n}<\frac1{72}\Bigl(\frac27\Bigr)^{\!n}.
\]

---

### 3.  Separate even and odd \(n\)

*If \(n\) is even* (\((-1)^{n}=+1\))  

\[
D<\frac1{72}\Bigl(\frac27\Bigr)^{\!n}.
\]

Since \(\bigl(\frac27\bigr)^{n}\downarrow 0\) as \(n\to\infty\), the strongest
requirement is  

\[
D\le 0 .
\]

*If \(n\) is odd* (\((-1)^{n}=-1\))  

\[
-D<\frac1{72}\Bigl(\frac27\Bigr)^{\!n}
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
D>-\frac1{72}\Bigl(\frac27\Bigr)^{\!n}.
\]

The tightest bound occurs for the smallest odd \(n\), i.e. \(n=1\):

\[
D>-\frac1{72}\cdot\frac27 =-\frac1{252}.
\]

Hence  

\[
-\frac1{252}< D\le 0 .
\]

---

### 4.  Translate back to \(a_0\)

\[
a_0 = D+\frac19\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\frac19-\frac1{252}< a_0\le\frac19 .
\]

Thus  

\[
a_0\in\Bigl(\frac{3}{28},\;\frac19\Bigr].
\]

All numbers in this interval make the sequence strictly increasing for
every \(n\ge 1\).

---

### 5.  Minimal possible value of \(\displaystyle\frac1{a_0}\)

Since \(a_0>0\), \(\frac1{a_0}\) decreases as \(a_0\) increases.
The largest admissible \(a_0\) is the right‑hand endpoint \(a_0=\frac19\).

\[
\boxed{\displaystyle\min\frac1{a_0}= \frac1{\,1/9\,}=9 }.
\]

Indeed, for \(a_0=\frac19\) the closed form reduces to \(a_n=\frac19\,2^{n}\),
which clearly satisfies \(a_{n+1}>a_n\) for all \(n\ge1\). No larger \(a_0\)
is allowed, so the minimal possible value of \(\frac1{a_0}\) is **9**.

