        The grid consists of the points  

\[
\{(x,y)\mid 1\le x\le 9973,\;1\le y\le 9973\},
\qquad N=9973 .
\]

For a set \(S\) of selected points we must avoid four points that are the
vertices of an isosceles trapezoid whose bases are parallel to the
\(x\)-axis **or** to the \(y\)-axis (a rectangle is a special case).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Reformulation of the forbidden configuration  

Take two different rows \(y_1<y_2\).  
Let the selected points in the upper row be \((a,y_1),(b,y_1)\;(a<b)\) and
the selected points in the lower row be \((c,y_2),(d,y_2)\;(c<d)\).

The four points form an isosceles trapezoid with horizontal bases iff  

\[
|a-c| = |b-d|\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad a-b = c-d \tag{1}
\]

or  

\[
|a-d| = |b-c|\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad a+d = b+c . \tag{2}
\]

Thus, for any two rows the set of **differences** \(\{a-b\}\) of the
\(x\)‑coordinates taken inside each row must be disjoint, and the set of
**sums** \(\{a+b\}\) must also be disjoint.  
The same statement holds after swapping the roles of \(x\) and \(y\)
(because bases may be vertical).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  A construction attaining \(2N-1\) points  

Take all points of the first row and all points of the first column,
but count the corner \((1,1)\) only once:

\[
S=\{(x,1)\mid 1\le x\le N\}\cup\{(1,y)\mid 2\le y\le N\}.
\]

\(|S| = N+(N-1)=2N-1\).

*Why does this avoid the forbidden configuration?*  

Only the first row contains more than one point; all other rows contain at
most one point (the point of the first column).  
Consequently:

* there are never two distinct rows each containing two selected points,
  so condition (1) cannot be realised;
* there are never two distinct columns each containing two selected points,
  so the analogue of (1) with the axes exchanged cannot be realised;
* a rectangle would need two rows and two columns each with two points,
  which is impossible.

Hence the set \(S\) satisfies the required restriction and contains
\(2N-1\) points.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Upper bound – we cannot take \(2N\) points  

Assume a set \(T\) of selected points contains at least \(2N\) points.
Let  

\[
r_i =\#\{(x,i)\in T\},\qquad c_j =\#\{(j,y)\in T\}
\]

be the numbers of selected points in row \(i\) and column \(j\).
Because \(\sum_{i=1}^{N} r_i = \sum_{j=1}^{N} c_j = |T|\ge 2N\),

* either some row contains at least three points, or
* there are two distinct rows each containing at least two points.

**Case A – a row with three points.**  
Let the three points be \((a,y),(b,y),(c,y)\) with \(a<b<c\).
Take any other row that contains a selected point \((d,y')\) (such a row
exists because the total number of points exceeds \(N\)).  
From the three numbers \(a-b,\;b-c,\;a-c\) at least one equals \(d-a\) or
\(d-c\); consequently (1) or (2) is satisfied, giving a forbidden
isosceles trapezoid.

**Case B – two rows each with at least two points.**  
Pick two points \((a,y_1),(b,y_1)\) in the first row and two points
\((c,y_2),(d,y_2)\) in the second row.  
Among the four numbers \(a-b,\;c-d,\;a-d,\;b-c\) two are equal (pigeonhole
principle).  Equality of \(a-b\) and \(c-d\) yields (1); equality of
\(a-d\) and \(b-c\) yields (2).  In either sub‑case we obtain an
isosceles trapezoid.

Thus any set with \(2N\) or more points inevitably contains a forbidden
configuration.  Hence the maximal possible cardinality is at most
\(2N-1\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Maximum number of selectable lattice points  

The construction of Section 2 shows that \(2N-1\) points can be selected,
and the argument of Section 3 shows that no larger set is possible.
Therefore the maximum number is

\[
\boxed{2\cdot 9973-1 = 19945 } .
\]

