--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  A construction with \(2N-1\) points  

Take  

* all points of the first row \((x,1),\;x=1,\dots ,N\);
* for every other row \(y=2,\dots ,N\) take the point \((1,y)\).

The set contains  

\[
N\;(\text{first row})\;+\;(N-1)\;(\text{first column, without the point }(1,1))
=2N-1
\]

points.

*Any four selected points would have to lie in two rows and two columns.
The only row that contains two points is the first row, and the only column
that contains two points is the first column.  
Hence we can never obtain two rows each with two selected points, nor two
columns each with two selected points, so no four points can form an
isosceles trapezoid whose bases are parallel to the axes.  
Thus \(2N-1\) points can indeed be selected.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Why we cannot select more than \(2N-1\) points  

Assume a selection contains more than \(2N-1\) points.

*If every row contained at most one selected point*, the total number of
selected points would be at most \(N\); therefore at least one row must
contain **at least two** selected points.  
Let  

\[
R=\{y\mid\text{row }y\text{ contains at least two selected points}\}.
\]

Similarly define  

\[
C=\{x\mid\text{column }x\text{ contains at least two selected points}\}.
\]

Because the total number of points exceeds \(2N-1\),

\[
|R|+|C|\ge 3 .
\tag{1}
\]

Indeed, if \(|R|\le 1\) and \(|C|\le 1\) then at most one row and one column
could have two points, giving at most  

\[
N\;(\text{one full row})\;+\;(N-1)\;(\text{one extra column})=2N-1
\]

points, contradicting the assumption.

Consequently at least two rows, say \(y_1\neq y_2\), belong to \(R\), and at
least two columns, say \(x_1\neq x_2\), belong to \(C\).

Choose in row \(y_1\) two selected points \((a_1,y_1),(b_1,y_1)\) and in
row \(y_2\) two selected points \((a_2,y_2),(b_2,y_2)\).
Set  

\[
d_1=a_1-b_1,\qquad d_2=a_2-b_2 .
\]

Because the two rows are different, the condition of the problem forces
\(d_1\neq d_2\) and also \(a_1+b_1\neq a_2+b_2\).

Now look at the two columns \(x_1,x_2\) that belong to \(C\).  
Each of them contains at least two selected points, hence provides a pair
of row‑coordinates whose difference is some integer \(e\) and whose sum is
some integer \(s\).  
Since there are only \(2N-2\) possible non‑zero differences and
\(2N-1\) possible sums, among the pairs taken from the two rows and the
two columns two equal differences (or two equal sums) must appear.
That equality yields exactly the situation described in the statement:
four selected points form an isosceles trapezoid whose bases are parallel
to the \(x\)- or \(y\)-axis – a contradiction.

Therefore a selection with more than \(2N-1\) points cannot exist.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Maximum number of selectable lattice points  

The construction shows that \(2N-1\) points are attainable, and the
argument above proves that no larger set is possible. Hence the maximum
possible number of selected lattice points is  

\[
\boxed{2\cdot 9973-1 = 19945}.
\]

