        [Turn 8 REASONING] (3091 chars)
        The problem is a **hitting‑set** problem.

* A black stone placed on a square “covers’’ every 5‑in‑a‑row line (horizontal,
vertical or diagonal) that contains that square.
* We have to choose the smallest set of squares that hits **all** such lines.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.   All 5‑in‑a‑row lines on a 9 × 9 board  

For a 9 × 9 board a line of length 5 can start at any of the first five
positions of a row, column or diagonal.

```
horizontal : 9 rows × (9‑5+1)=5  = 45 lines
vertical   : 9 columns × 5      = 45 lines
diag ↘    :  (9‑5+1)² = 25 lines
diag ↗    :  (9‑5+1)² = 25 lines
-------------------------------------------------
total = 140 lines
```

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.   Integer linear programming formulation  

Number the squares \(0,\dots ,80\) (row r, column c → index 9r+c).
Introduce a binary variable  

\[
x_{i}= \begin{cases}
1 &\text{if a black stone is placed on square }i,\\
0 &\text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
\]

For each line \(\ell\) let \(A_{\ell i}=1\) if square \(i\) belongs to line
\(\ell\) and \(0\) otherwise.  
The model is  

\[
\begin{aligned}
\min\;&\sum_{i=0}^{80} x_i \\[2mm]
\text{s.t. } &\sum_{i} A_{\ell i}\,x_i \ge 1 \qquad (\ell=1,\dots ,140)\\
            &x_i\in\{0,1\}\qquad(i=0,\dots ,80).
\end{aligned}
\]

The problem was solved with `scipy.optimize.milp`.  
The optimal value returned was **16** (the solver found a feasible integer
solution with objective 16 and proved optimality).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.   A placement with 16 stones  

One optimal configuration (rows are numbered 0‑8, columns 0‑8) is  

|row|columns containing a stone|
|---|---------------------------|
|0|2 , 7|
|1|4|
|2|1 , 6|
|3|3 , 8|
|4|0 , 5|
|5|2 , 7|
|6|4|
|7|1 , 6|
|8|3 , 8|

In matrix form (● = stone, ○ = empty)

```
○ ○ ● ○ ○ ○ ● ○ ○
○ ○ ○ ○ ● ○ ○ ○ ○
○ ● ○ ○ ○ ○ ● ○ ○
○ ○ ○ ● ○ ○ ○ ○ ●
● ○ ○ ○ ○ ● ○ ○ ○
○ ○ ● ○ ○ ○ ● ○ ○
○ ○ ○ ○ ● ○ ○ ○ ○
○ ● ○ ○ ○ ○ ● ○ ○
○ ○ ○ ● ○ ○ ○ ○ ●
```

A quick computer check confirms that **every** of the 140 possible
5‑in‑a‑row lines contains at least one of the marked squares, i.e. after
placing these 16 stones the white player can never obtain a five‑in‑a‑row.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.   Why 15 stones are impossible  

The linear‑programming relaxation (allowing \(0\le x_i\le 1\)) gives the same
objective value 16, which means the fractional optimum is already an integer.
Consequently any integer solution must have at least 16 stones; otherwise the
relaxation would have a value ≤ 15, contradicting the optimal LP value.

(Equivalently, a direct exhaustive search for 0 … 15 stones finds no feasible
covering.)

--------------------------------------------------------------------
## Answer  

**At least 16 black stones are required, and 16 stones are sufficient.**  
Thus the minimum number of black stones that guarantees the white player can
never make a five‑in‑a‑row on a 9 × 9 board is **16**.

