Triggers: x^2 - Dy^2 = 1 where D is non-square, integer points on hyperbola, rational approximations to sqrt(D), find smallest solution to quadratic diophantine
Technique: Pell equation x^2-Dy^2=1: compute continued fraction expansion of sqrt(D), build convergents p_k/q_k. Fundamental solution (x_1,y_1) is first convergent satisfying the equation. All solutions come from (x_n+y_n*sqrt(D))=(x_1+y_1*sqrt(D))^n. For x^2-Dy^2=-1, solution exists iff CF period is odd. For x^2-Dy^2=k, use fundamental solution and norm multiplication.
