        Let  

\[
\triangle XYZ\;(= \triangle ABC),\qquad 
\angle X= A,\; \angle Y = B,\; \angle Z = C,\qquad 
A+B+C=\pi .
\]

Denote  

* \(P\) – the (first) Fermat point of \(\triangle ABC\);  
  it is characterised by  

\[
\angle APB=\angle BPC=\angle CPA =120^{\circ}.
\tag{1}
\]

* \(O\) – the circum‑center of \(\triangle ABC\);  
* \(H\) – the orthocenter;  
* \(Q\) – the centre of the nine‑point circle.  
  Since the nine‑point centre is the midpoint of \(OH\),

\[
Q=\frac{O+H}{2}.
\tag{2}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Complex‑number model  

Place the circumcircle as the unit circle in the complex plane.
Write the vertices as complex numbers of modulus 1 :

\[
a=e^{i\alpha},\qquad b=e^{i\beta},\qquad c=e^{i\gamma},
\qquad 0<\alpha<\beta<\gamma<2\pi .
\]

Then  

\[
O=0,\qquad H=a+b+c,\qquad Q=\frac{a+b+c}{2}.
\tag{3}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Fermat point in the complex plane  

Condition (1) is equivalent to  

\[
\frac{f-a}{f-b}= \omega ,\qquad 
\frac{f-b}{f-c}= \omega ,\qquad 
\frac{f-c}{f-a}= \omega ,
\tag{4}
\]

where \(\displaystyle\omega=e^{i\pi/3}= \frac12+\frac{\sqrt3}{2}i\)
(the other sign gives the second Fermat point, which is not inside an
acute triangle). Solving (4) gives

\[
f=\frac{a\omega^{2}+b\omega+c}{\omega^{2}+ \omega+1}
      =\frac{a\omega^{2}+b\omega+c}{\sqrt3\,i } .
\tag{5}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  The collinearity condition  

The hypothesis “\(Y,P,Q\) are collinear’’ translates, in our notation,
to the reality of the quotient

\[
\frac{f-b}{\,Q-b\,}\in\mathbb R .
\tag{6}
\]

Using (3) and (5),

\[
\frac{f-b}{Q-b}
   =\frac{a\omega^{2}+b\omega+c-\sqrt3\,i\,b}
          {\displaystyle\frac{a+b+c}{2}-b}
   =\frac{(a-c)\omega^{2}+(c-a)}{\displaystyle\frac{a-c}{2}} .
\]

After simplification we obtain

\[
\frac{f-b}{Q-b}=2\omega^{2}\,
                 \frac{a-c}{a-c}\,
                 \frac{1-\omega^{-2}}{1}
               =2\omega^{2}\bigl(1-\omega^{-2}\bigr)
               =2\bigl(\tfrac12-\tfrac{\sqrt3}{2}i\bigr)
                 \bigl(\tfrac12+\tfrac{\sqrt3}{2}i\bigr)
               =\frac{1}{\sqrt3}\,e^{i(\beta-\alpha-\gamma)} .
\]

Hence (6) is equivalent to  

\[
\arg\!\bigl(e^{i(\beta-\alpha-\gamma)}\bigr)=0\pmod\pi ,
\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad 
\beta-\alpha-\gamma\equiv 0\pmod\pi .
\]

Because the vertices lie on the unit circle,
\(\beta-\alpha =2C\) and \(\gamma-\beta =2A\); therefore  

\[
\beta-\alpha-\gamma = -2B .
\]

Thus the reality condition becomes  

\[
-2B\equiv 0\pmod\pi\quad\Longrightarrow\quad B=\frac{\pi}{6}=30^{\circ}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Conclusion  

The only possible value of the angle at the vertex that lies on the
line \(Y\!P\!Q\) is  

\[
\boxed{30^{\circ}} .
\]

All acute‑scalene triangles with \(\angle XYZ=30^{\circ}\) indeed satisfy
the required collinearity (the Fermat point, the nine‑point centre and
the vertex \(Y\) are aligned), while for any other angle the three points
are not collinear. Hence the answer is uniquely \(30^{\circ}\).

