        [Turn 8 REASONING] (1963 chars)
        The condition forces the triangle to be **isosceles**.

Let the angles of \(\triangle XYZ\) be  
\[
\angle X = A,\qquad \angle Y = B,\qquad \angle Z = C,
\qquad A+B+C=180^{\circ}.
\]

\(P\) is the first Fermat point, so its trilinear coordinates are  

\[
\csc\!\left(A+\frac{60^{\circ}}{}\right):\csc\!\left(B+\frac{60^{\circ}}{}\right):
\csc\!\left(C+\frac{60^{\circ}}{}\right).
\]

The circle through the three side‑midpoints has centre \(Q\), the nine‑point centre,
whose barycentric coordinates are  

\[
a\cos A : b\cos B : c\cos C .
\]

The collinearity of \(Y\,(0,1,0),\;P\) and \(Q\) gives  

\[
\det\!\begin{pmatrix}
0&1&0\\[2pt]
a\cos A & b\cos B & c\cos C\\[2pt]
a\csc\!\left(A+60^{\circ}\right) &
b\csc\!\left(B+60^{\circ}\right) &
c\csc\!\left(C+60^{\circ}\right)
\end{pmatrix}=0 .
\]

Cancelling the common factors \(a,c\) we obtain  

\[
\cos A\,\sin\!\left(A+60^{\circ}\right)=
\cos C\,\sin\!\left(C+60^{\circ}\right).
\]

Using \(\sin(x+60^{\circ})=\tfrac12\sin x+\tfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\cos x\) this reduces to  

\[
\cos A\,\sin A+\sqrt3\cos^{2}A=
\cos C\,\sin C+\sqrt3\cos^{2}C .
\]

Hence  

\[
\sin 2A- \sin 2C =2\sqrt3\bigl(\cos^{2}C-\cos^{2}A\bigr)
\Longrightarrow
