cancel it, obtaining  

\[
\cos(A+C)=\sqrt3\sin(A+C)\;\Longrightarrow\;
\tan(A+C)=\frac1{\sqrt3}= \tan30^{\circ}.
\]

Thus \(A+C=30^{\circ}\) or \(A+C=210^{\circ}\); the latter is impossible for an
acute triangle. Consequently  

\[
A+C=30^{\circ}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad B=180^{\circ}-(A+C)=150^{\circ},
\]

which contradicts the hypothesis that the triangle is acute.

Hence **no acute‑scalene triangle can satisfy the condition**; the only
possibilities are the isosceles case \(A=C\) (so \(XY=YZ\)), for which any acute
value of \(\angle XYZ\) is allowed.

